-<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="theme-color" content="#125e58"><title>Brain anatomy: regions, ventricles and pathways | Corpus</title><meta name="description" content="Midsagittal section: cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem and cerebellum around the ventricular system."><meta name="robots" content="noindex,follow"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:title" content="Brain anatomy: regions, ventricles and pathways"><meta property="og:description" content="Midsagittal section: cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem and cerebellum around the ventricular system."><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><link rel="icon" href="../../favicon.svg"><link rel="stylesheet" href="../../editorial.css?v=48"></head><body><a class="skip" href="#content">Skip to content</a><header class="site-head"><div class="shell head-inner"><a class="brand" href="../../index.html" aria-label="Corpus Anatomy Atlas home"><span class="brand-mark">♥</span><span class="brand-name">Corpus<small>Anatomy Atlas</small></span></a><nav class="head-nav" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="../../learn/" >Study guides</a><a href="../../index.html" >Interactive atlas</a><a href="../../index.html#/map" >Body map</a><a href="../../index.html#/quiz" >Quiz</a><a href="../../about.html" >About us</a></nav></div></header><div class="reading-track" aria-hidden="true"><span id="reading-progress"></span></div><nav class="crumb shell" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="../../index.html">Home</a><i>/</i><a href="../">Study guides</a><i>/</i><span>Brain</span></nav><section class="shell hero article-hero"><div class="eyebrow">Nervous · illustrated anatomy</div><h1>Brain anatomy: regions, ventricles and pathways</h1><p>Midsagittal section: cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem and cerebellum around the ventricular system.</p><div class="action-row"><a class="button" href="../../index.html#/brain">Open interactive atlas ↗</a><a class="button alt" href="#parts">Read the structures ↓</a></div><p class="byline">Corpus educational reference · 20 documented study entries · Updated 2026-10-18</p></section><div class="shell article-grid"><main id="content"><figure class="plate"><img src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" alt="Digital educational or regional anatomy illustration for Brain; " width="500" height="500" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><figcaption>Regional teaching illustration.  Preview a part for a more relevant close-up when available; only identifiable structures receive a pointer.</figcaption></figure><section id="overview"><div class="eyebrow">Overview</div><h2>How this part of the body works</h2><p class="intro-text">The brain integrates sensation, generates movement, and supports language, memory and consciousness. It uses about 20% of the body&#x27;s oxygen and glucose despite being 2% of body weight, and it has almost no energy reserve — four minutes of ischaemia starts to kill neurons. The cerebral cortex is folded into gyri and sulci to pack ~0.15 m² of grey matter into the skull. Beneath it, white-matter tracts such as the corpus callosum connect the hemispheres, while the diencephalon and brainstem handle relay, arousal and the automatic control of breathing, heart rate and blood pressure.</p><div class="facts"><div class="fact"><b>Body system</b><span>Nervous</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Location</b><span>Cranial cavity, above the brainstem</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Blood supply</b><span>Internal carotid + vertebral arteries → circle of Willis (15% of cardiac output)</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Innervation</b><span>Itself the source of cranial nerves I–XII</span></div></div></section><section id="parts" class="content-section"><div class="eyebrow">Illustrated structures</div><h2>Read the anatomy, part by part</h2><p class="section-lead">These 17 structures describe anatomy related to the chapter’s regional illustration. Each entry separates location, function and a clinical point.</p><div class="part-list"><section class="part" id="part-cerebrum"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Cerebrum" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="700" data-point-y="300" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Cerebrum"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brain teaching plate: Cerebrum" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:70.0% 30.0%"></button><span class="number">01</span><h3>Cerebrum</h3></div><p>The largest part of the brain: two hemispheres of folded cortex over deep white matter, basal ganglia and the lateral ventricles.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Voluntary movement, sensation, language, memory, personality and conscious thought.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Its surface folding (gyri and sulci) triples cortical area; abnormal folding causes lissencephaly and epilepsy.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-frontal"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Frontal lobe" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Frontal lobe"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brain; Frontal lobe is not separately marked" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">02</span><h3>Frontal lobe</h3></div><small>Lobus frontalis</small><p>Anterior to the central sulcus and above the lateral sulcus. Contains the primary motor cortex, Broca&#x27;s area (usually left) and the prefrontal cortex.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Planning, judgement, personality, voluntary movement and expressive speech.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Prefrontal damage (classically Phineas Gage) changes personality and impulse control while sparing memory and IQ.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-parietal"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Parietal lobe" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Parietal lobe"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brain; Parietal lobe is not separately marked" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">03</span><h3>Parietal lobe</h3></div><small>Lobus parietalis</small><p>Behind the central sulcus, above the temporal lobe. Houses the primary somatosensory cortex in the postcentral gyrus.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Processing of touch, pain, temperature, position sense and spatial awareness.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Right-sided lesions cause hemispatial neglect — the patient ignores the whole left side of space.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-occipital"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Occipital lobe" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Occipital lobe"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brain; Occipital lobe is not separately marked" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">04</span><h3>Occipital lobe</h3></div><small>Lobus occipitalis</small><p>The posterior pole, bounded medially by the parieto-occipital sulcus, containing the primary visual cortex on the banks of the calcarine sulcus.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Vision — the retinal image is mapped point-for-point on the calcarine cortex.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A posterior cerebral artery stroke causes a congruous homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-temporal"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Temporal lobe" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Temporal lobe"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brain; Temporal lobe is not separately marked" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">05</span><h3>Temporal lobe</h3></div><small>Lobus temporalis</small><p>Below the lateral sulcus, including the hippocampal formation and primary auditory cortex on the superior temporal gyrus.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Hearing, memory formation, language comprehension (Wernicke&#x27;s area) and emotion.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>The medial temporal lobe is the commonest focus of temporal-lobe epilepsy and the earliest region damaged in Alzheimer disease.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-corpus"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Corpus callosum" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="640" data-point-y="389" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Corpus callosum"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brain teaching plate: Corpus callosum" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:64.0% 38.9%"></button><span class="number">06</span><h3>Corpus callosum</h3></div><p>The great C-shaped commissure of ~200 million myelinated fibres: rostrum, genu, body and splenium.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Connects matching regions of the two hemispheres so they work as one brain.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Surgically sectioned for intractable epilepsy, producing &quot;split-brain&quot; patients who cannot name an object felt in the left hand.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-latvent"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Lateral ventricle" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="600" data-point-y="333" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Lateral ventricle"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brain teaching plate: Lateral ventricle" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:60.0% 33.3%"></button><span class="number">07</span><h3>Lateral ventricle</h3></div><small>Ventriculus lateralis</small><p>C-shaped CSF cavity in each hemisphere with anterior, body, posterior and temporal horns, lined by ependyma with choroid plexus on the floor.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Cushions the brain and circulates CSF produced by the choroid plexus.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Obstruction of the interventricular foramen causes hydrocephalus and enlargement of the ventricle.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-thalamus"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Thalamus" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="544" data-point-y="456" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Thalamus"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brain teaching plate: Thalamus" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:54.4% 45.6%"></button><span class="number">08</span><h3>Thalamus</h3></div><p>Two ovals of grey matter flanking the third ventricle, subdivided into anterior, medial and lateral nuclear groups.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>The relay station: nearly all sensory information (except smell) is edited and sent on to the cortex. Also involved in arousal.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A thalamic stroke can produce severe contralateral sensory loss and thalamic pain syndrome.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-hypothalamus"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Hypothalamus" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="524" data-point-y="486" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Hypothalamus"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brain teaching plate: Hypothalamus" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:52.4% 48.6%"></button><span class="number">09</span><h3>Hypothalamus</h3></div><p>Only 4 g, yet it controls the autonomic and endocrine systems: supraoptic, paraventricular, arcuate and suprachiasmatic nuclei.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Temperature, thirst, hunger, circadian rhythm, and hormone release via the pituitary.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Lesions cause diabetes insipidus, obesity or emaciation, and disruption of sleep-wake cycles.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-pituitary"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Pituitary gland" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Pituitary gland"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brain; Pituitary gland is not separately marked" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">10</span><h3>Pituitary gland</h3></div><small>Hypophysis</small><p>Sits in the sella turcica below the hypothalamus, joined by the infundibulum. Anterior lobe is glandular; posterior lobe is neural tissue.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Secretes GH, TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH, prolactin, plus oxytocin and vasopressin released from the posterior lobe.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A pituitary adenoma pressing upward on the optic chiasm causes bitemporal hemianopia — loss of the outer half of both visual fields.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-midbrain"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Midbrain" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="540" data-point-y="556" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Midbrain"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brain teaching plate: Midbrain" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:54.0% 55.6%"></button><span class="number">11</span><h3>Midbrain</h3></div><small>Mesencephalon</small><p>Tectum (superior and inferior colliculi) plus cerebral peduncles, with the cerebral aqueduct running through it.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Visual and auditory reflexes, eye movements, and the dopamine-producing substantia nigra.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Degeneration of the substantia nigra causes Parkinson disease: tremor, rigidity and bradykinesia.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-pons"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Pons" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="510" data-point-y="644" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Pons"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brain teaching plate: Pons" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:51.0% 64.4%"></button><span class="number">12</span><h3>Pons</h3></div><p>The bulging bridge anterior to the cerebellum, carrying transverse fibres into the middle cerebellar peduncles.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Relays cortex to cerebellum; holds the pneumotaxic and apneustic centres that shape breathing, plus nuclei of cranial nerves V–VIII.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Occlusion of the basilar artery can cause locked-in syndrome — full awareness with paralysis of everything except vertical eye movement.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-medulla"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Medulla oblongata" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="480" data-point-y="733" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Medulla oblongata"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brain teaching plate: Medulla oblongata" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:48.0% 73.3%"></button><span class="number">13</span><h3>Medulla oblongata</h3></div><p>The lowest brainstem segment, continuous with the spinal cord at the foramen magnum. Contains the pyramids, olives and the floor of the fourth ventricle.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Cardiac, vasomotor and respiratory centres; reflexes for vomiting, coughing and swallowing. The pyramidal decussation happens here.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Because motor fibres cross at the medulla, a lesion above the decussation paralyses the opposite side of the body.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-cerebellum"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Cerebellum" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="340" data-point-y="611" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Cerebellum"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brain teaching plate: Cerebellum" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:34.0% 61.1%"></button><span class="number">14</span><h3>Cerebellum</h3></div><p>Two hemispheres and a midline vermis, with a densely folded cortex, deep nuclei (dentate, emboliform, globose, fastigial) and branching white matter — the arbor vitae.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Compares intended movement with actual movement and corrects it: coordination, precision, timing, posture and motor learning.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Damage causes ipsilateral ataxia, intention tremor, dysdiadochokinesia and a broad-based gait. Alcohol affects it first.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-fourth"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Fourth ventricle" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Fourth ventricle"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brain; Fourth ventricle is not separately marked" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">15</span><h3>Fourth ventricle</h3></div><small>Ventriculus quartus</small><p>A diamond-shaped cavity between the pons/medulla in front and the cerebellum behind, draining through the foramina of Luschka and Magendie.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>CSF passes from here into the subarachnoid space around brain and spinal cord.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Blocked outflow causes obstructive hydrocephalus — a common presentation of posterior fossa tumours in children.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-spinalcord"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Spinal cord" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="460" data-point-y="866" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Spinal cord"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brain teaching plate: Spinal cord" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:46.0% 86.6%"></button><span class="number">16</span><h3>Spinal cord</h3></div><small>Medulla spinalis</small><p>Continues from the medulla at the foramen magnum and ends at L1/L2 as the conus medullaris, with the cauda equina below.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Carries sensory information up and motor commands down, and runs reflex arcs independently of the brain.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Transection above C3–C5 stops diaphragmatic breathing; at thoracic level it causes paraplegia with a sensory level.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-gyri"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brain.webp" data-part-name="Gyri &amp; sulci" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="500" data-point-y="240" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Gyri &amp; sulci"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brain teaching plate: Gyri &amp; sulci" width="500" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 24.0%"></button><span class="number">17</span><h3>Gyri &amp; sulci</h3></div><small>Gyri et sulci</small><p>The folded ridges and grooves of the cortex. Grey matter (neuron cell bodies) is 2–4 mm thick on the surface; white matter (myelinated axons) lies beneath.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Folding multiplies cortical surface area about threefold within the fixed volume of the skull.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>The pattern is individually unique and is disrupted in cortical dysplasias that cause focal epilepsy.</p></div></section></div></section><section id="deep" class="content-section"><div class="eyebrow">Beyond the visible plate</div><h2>Deeper structures and tissue layers</h2><p class="section-lead">These 3 additional entries give anatomical context. They are deliberately not assigned a photographic pin on the regional image.</p><p class=note>Related deep chapters: <a href="../cerebraldeep/">Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways</a>, <a href="../meningescsf/">Meninges, CSF &amp; venous sinuses</a>, <a href="../brainstemdeep/">Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry</a>.</p><div class="part-list"><section class="part" id="part-ctx_cerebraldeep_corticalgraymatter"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Cortical gray matter" data-preview-kind="detail" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="290" data-point-y="260" data-reference-href="/learn/cerebraldeep/" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Cortical gray matter"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Detail on the Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways teaching plate: Cortical gray matter" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:29.0% 26.0%"></button><span class="number">18</span><h3>Cortical gray matter</h3></div><small>Substantia grisea corticalis</small><p>Neuronal cell-body sheet folded into gyri and sulci.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Integrates sensory, motor and association information.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Regional cortical lesions give different deficits.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-ctx_meningescsf_periostealcranialdura"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/meninges-csf.webp" data-part-name="Periosteal cranial dura" data-preview-kind="detail" data-preview-context="true" data-reference-href="/learn/meningescsf/" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Periosteal cranial dura"><img src="../../img/thumb/meninges-csf.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Meninges, CSF &amp; venous sinuses; Periosteal cranial dura is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">19</span><h3>Periosteal cranial dura</h3></div><small>Stratum periostale durae</small><p>Outer cranial dural layer adherent to inner skull.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Forms tough outer covering and part of sinus walls.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Spinal epidural space differs from cranial periosteal interface.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-ctx_brainstemdeep_superiorcolliculus"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Superior colliculus" data-preview-kind="detail" data-preview-context="true" data-reference-href="/learn/brainstemdeep/" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Superior colliculus"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry; Superior colliculus is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">20</span><h3>Superior colliculus</h3></div><small>Colliculus superior</small><p>Dorsal midbrain paired structure.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Coordinates orienting eye/head movements to visual stimuli.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Not the origin of the optic nerve.</p></div></section></div></section><section id="questions" class="content-section questions"><div class="eyebrow">Questions people ask</div><h2>Five questions about Brain</h2><p class="section-lead">Short answers grounded in the structures described above. Use the illustrated guide for anatomical context.</p><div class="qa-list"><div class="qa-item"><h3>What are the main parts of the brain?</h3><p>The cerebrum supports complex thought, sensation and voluntary movement; the cerebellum coordinates movement; and the brainstem connects the brain to the spinal cord and helps regulate essential functions.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>What protects the brain inside the skull?</h3><p>The skull, three meningeal layers and cerebrospinal fluid provide physical protection. Blood vessels supply its metabolically active tissue.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>What is the frontal lobe?</h3><p>Anterior to the central sulcus and above the lateral sulcus. Contains the primary motor cortex, Broca&#x27;s area (usually left) and the prefrontal cortex. Function: Planning, judgement, personality, voluntary movement and expressive speech.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>Where is the thalamus located?</h3><p>Two ovals of grey matter flanking the third ventricle, subdivided into anterior, medial and lateral nuclear groups. Function: The relay station: nearly all sensory information (except smell) is edited and sent on to the cortex. Also involved in arousal.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>What is the function of the cerebellum?</h3><p>Function: Compares intended movement with actual movement and corrects it: coordination, precision, timing, posture and motor learning. Two hemispheres and a midline vermis, with a densely folded cortex, deep nuclei (dentate, emboliform, globose, fastigial) and branching white matter — the arbor vitae.</p></div></div></section><section id="related" class="content-section related-section"><div class="eyebrow">Keep exploring</div><h2>Related anatomy guides</h2><p class="section-lead">Follow these connected structures and body systems in the illustrated atlas.</p><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="../cerebraldeep/"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways</strong><small>Trace cortex, white matter, corpus callosum, basal nuclei and thalamus on a realistic coronal section.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../meningescsf/"><img src="../../img/thumb/meninges-csf.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Meninges, CSF &amp; venous sinuses" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Meninges, CSF &amp; venous sinuses</strong><small>Follow dura, arachnoid and pia; then CSF from choroid plexus to subarachnoid drainage.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../brainstemdeep/"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry</strong><small>Locate brainstem levels, cranial-nerve nuclei, pyramids and cerebellar peduncles, cortex and nuclei.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../spinalcord/"><img src="../../img/thumb/spinalcord.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Spinal cord" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Spinal cord</strong><small>Cross-section: a grey butterfly inside white tracts, wrapped in three meninges.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a></div></section><section id="sources" class="content-section sources"><div class="eyebrow">Reference desk</div><h2>Further reading</h2><p class="section-lead">General anatomy references and related source chapters for continued study. These links do not make the digital illustrations clinical images or certify every anatomical variant.</p><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK10926/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ↗</a></li></ul><p class="disclaimer">This educational page is not medical advice and has not undergone formal clinical peer review. Spotted an error? <a href="../../about.html#contact">Contact the editor</a>.</p></section><div class="end-link"><div><strong>See the structures in context</strong><p>Open the realistic interactive plate, label lines, focus view and self-test.</p></div><a class="button" href="../../index.html#/brain">Open interactive view →</a></div></main><aside class="sidebar" aria-label="On this page"><div class="side-card"><h2>In this guide</h2><a href="#overview">Overview</a><a href="#parts">Illustrated structures (17)</a><a href="#deep">Deep study notes (3)</a><a href="#questions">Five questions &amp; answers</a><a href="#related">Related guides</a><a href="#sources">Further reading</a></div><div class="side-card"><div class="count">20+</div><p>Documented anatomy and developmental entries in every Corpus chapter. Images are orientation aids, not clinical evidence.</p></div></aside></div><footer class="site-foot"><div class="shell foot-inner"><div><strong>Corpus Anatomy Atlas</strong><p>Illustrated anatomy for learning, not a diagnostic or surgical reference. Digital renders explain regions; they are not clinical photographs or substitutes for histology slides.</p></div><nav class="foot-links" aria-label="Footer"><a href="../../learn/">Study guides</a><a href="../../index.html">Interactive atlas</a><a href="../../about.html">About &amp; editorial policy</a><a href="../../privacy.html">Privacy</a></nav></div></footer><script defer src="../../reading.js?v=33"></script><script defer src="../../preview.js?v=48"></script></body></html>
