-<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="theme-color" content="#125e58"><title>Brainstem and cerebellum anatomy | Corpus</title><meta name="description" content="Locate brainstem levels, cranial-nerve nuclei, pyramids and cerebellar peduncles, cortex and nuclei."><meta name="robots" content="noindex,follow"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:title" content="Brainstem and cerebellum anatomy"><meta property="og:description" content="Locate brainstem levels, cranial-nerve nuclei, pyramids and cerebellar peduncles, cortex and nuclei."><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><link rel="icon" href="../../favicon.svg"><link rel="stylesheet" href="../../editorial.css?v=48"></head><body><a class="skip" href="#content">Skip to content</a><header class="site-head"><div class="shell head-inner"><a class="brand" href="../../index.html" aria-label="Corpus Anatomy Atlas home"><span class="brand-mark">♥</span><span class="brand-name">Corpus<small>Anatomy Atlas</small></span></a><nav class="head-nav" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="../../learn/" >Study guides</a><a href="../../index.html" >Interactive atlas</a><a href="../../index.html#/map" >Body map</a><a href="../../index.html#/quiz" >Quiz</a><a href="../../about.html" >About us</a></nav></div></header><div class="reading-track" aria-hidden="true"><span id="reading-progress"></span></div><nav class="crumb shell" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="../../index.html">Home</a><i>/</i><a href="../">Study guides</a><i>/</i><span>Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry</span></nav><section class="shell hero article-hero"><div class="eyebrow">Nervous · illustrated anatomy</div><h1>Brainstem and cerebellum anatomy</h1><p>Locate brainstem levels, cranial-nerve nuclei, pyramids and cerebellar peduncles, cortex and nuclei.</p><div class="action-row"><a class="button" href="../../index.html#/brainstemdeep">Open interactive atlas ↗</a><a class="button alt" href="#parts">Read the structures ↓</a></div><p class="byline">Corpus educational reference · 20 documented study entries · Updated 2026-10-18</p></section><div class="shell article-grid"><main id="content"><figure class="plate"><img src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Digital educational or regional anatomy illustration for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry; " width="1408" height="768" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><figcaption>Regional teaching illustration.  Preview a part for a more relevant close-up when available; only identifiable structures receive a pointer.</figcaption></figure><section id="overview"><div class="eyebrow">Overview</div><h2>How this part of the body works</h2><p class="intro-text">Midbrain, pons and medulla form the brainstem, carrying ascending sensation and descending motor commands while housing many cranial nerve nuclei and arousal/autonomic networks. Dorsal to pons and medulla, cerebellum compares intended and actual movement through cortex and deep nuclei. Inferior, middle and superior cerebellar peduncles connect it to medulla, pons and midbrain respectively; traffic direction is not identical in each peduncle. Purkinje cells inhibit deep nuclei; cerebellar dysfunction causes coordination deficits rather than classic isolated paralysis.</p><div class="facts"><div class="fact"><b>Body system</b><span>Nervous</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Location</b><span>Midbrain → pons → medulla; cerebellum dorsal to pons/medulla</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Blood supply</b><span>Vertebrobasilar branches, PICA, AICA and superior cerebellar artery territories</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Innervation</b><span>Brainstem cranial nerve nuclei, ascending/descending tracts and cerebellar peduncles</span></div></div></section><section id="parts" class="content-section"><div class="eyebrow">Illustrated structures</div><h2>Read the anatomy, part by part</h2><p class="section-lead">These 19 structures describe anatomy related to the chapter’s regional illustration. Each entry separates location, function and a clinical point.</p><div class="part-list"><section class="part" id="part-midbrain"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Midbrain" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="430" data-point-y="185" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Midbrain"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry teaching plate: Midbrain" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:43.0% 18.5%"></button><span class="number">01</span><h3>Midbrain</h3></div><small>Mesencephalon</small><p>Rostral brainstem around cerebral aqueduct between diencephalon and pons.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Hosts III/IV-related nuclei and visual/auditory orienting circuits.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Midbrain lesions can combine ocular and long-tract signs.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-superiorcolliculus"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Superior colliculus" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Superior colliculus"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry; Superior colliculus is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">02</span><h3>Superior colliculus</h3></div><small>Colliculus superior</small><p>Dorsal midbrain paired structure.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Coordinates orienting eye/head movements to visual stimuli.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Not the origin of the optic nerve.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-inferiorcolliculus"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Inferior colliculus" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Inferior colliculus"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry; Inferior colliculus is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">03</span><h3>Inferior colliculus</h3></div><small>Colliculus inferior</small><p>Dorsal caudal midbrain auditory relay structure.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Integrates auditory pathway toward thalamus.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Auditory pathways are largely bilateral centrally.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-substantianigra"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Substantia nigra" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Substantia nigra"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry; Substantia nigra is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">04</span><h3>Substantia nigra</h3></div><p>Pigmented midbrain nuclei with pars compacta and reticulata.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Dopaminergic modulation and basal-ganglia output.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Degeneration of pars compacta occurs in Parkinson disease.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-cerebralpeduncles"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Cerebral peduncles" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Cerebral peduncles"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry; Cerebral peduncles is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">05</span><h3>Cerebral peduncles</h3></div><small>Pedunculi cerebri</small><p>Ventral midbrain bundles carrying major descending cortical fibers.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Transmit corticospinal, corticobulbar and corticopontine signals.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Ventral lesion may cause contralateral weakness.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-pons"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Pons" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="490" data-point-y="460" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Pons"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry teaching plate: Pons" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:49.0% 46.0%"></button><span class="number">06</span><h3>Pons</h3></div><p>Bulging middle brainstem with pontine nuclei and transverse fibers.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Relays cortico-ponto-cerebellar signals.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Facial and abducens pathways traverse pontine region.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-pontinenuclei"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Pontine nuclei" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Pontine nuclei"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry; Pontine nuclei is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">07</span><h3>Pontine nuclei</h3></div><small>Nuclei pontis</small><p>Ventral pontine relay receiving extensive cortical input.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Project contralaterally through middle cerebellar peduncle.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Connect cerebral plans to cerebellum.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-medullaoblongata"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Medulla oblongata" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="525" data-point-y="730" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Medulla oblongata"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry teaching plate: Medulla oblongata" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:52.5% 73.0%"></button><span class="number">08</span><h3>Medulla oblongata</h3></div><p>Caudal brainstem continuous with spinal cord.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Contains autonomic, swallowing and motor/sensory pathways.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Medullary injury can compromise respiration and circulation.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-medullarypyramids"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Medullary pyramids" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Medullary pyramids"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry; Medullary pyramids is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">09</span><h3>Medullary pyramids</h3></div><small>Pyramides medullae</small><p>Ventral medullary corticospinal bundles.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Major descending voluntary motor route.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Most fibers cross at caudal pyramidal decussation.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-inferiorolives"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Inferior olives" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Inferior olives"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry; Inferior olives is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">10</span><h3>Inferior olives</h3></div><small>Olivae medullae</small><p>Ventral-lateral medullary elevations containing inferior olivary complex.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Provide climbing-fiber teaching signals to cerebellum.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Lateral medullary lesions can affect nearby pathways.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-fourthventricle"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Fourth ventricle" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="600" data-point-y="470" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Fourth ventricle"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry teaching plate: Fourth ventricle" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:60.0% 47.0%"></button><span class="number">11</span><h3>Fourth ventricle</h3></div><small>Ventriculus quartus</small><p>Between dorsal pons/upper medulla and cerebellum.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Channels CSF into subarachnoid spaces.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Its floor is rhomboid fossa.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-cerebellarvermis"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Cerebellar vermis" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Cerebellar vermis"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry; Cerebellar vermis is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">12</span><h3>Cerebellar vermis</h3></div><small>Vermis cerebelli</small><p>Midline structure joining cerebellar hemispheres.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Coordinates trunk, posture and gait.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Midline disease often causes truncal ataxia.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-cerebellarhemispheres"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Cerebellar hemispheres" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="760" data-point-y="460" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Cerebellar hemispheres"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry teaching plate: Cerebellar hemispheres" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:76.0% 46.0%"></button><span class="number">13</span><h3>Cerebellar hemispheres</h3></div><small>Hemispheria cerebelli</small><p>Lateral folded portions of cerebellum.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Coordinate skilled limb timing and error correction.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Lesions usually cause ipsilateral limb ataxia.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-cerebellarcortex"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Cerebellar cortex" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="750" data-point-y="270" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Cerebellar cortex"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry teaching plate: Cerebellar cortex" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:75.0% 27.0%"></button><span class="number">14</span><h3>Cerebellar cortex</h3></div><small>Cortex cerebelli</small><p>Molecular, Purkinje-cell and granular layers on folia.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Transforms incoming signals into inhibitory Purkinje output.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Three-layer pattern differs from six-layer neocortex.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-purkinjecell"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Purkinje cell" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Purkinje cell"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry; Purkinje cell is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">15</span><h3>Purkinje cell</h3></div><small>Cellula Purkinje</small><p>Large inhibitory neuron forming a single cell-body row between molecular and granular cerebellar layers.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Sends GABAergic output to deep cerebellar and vestibular nuclei.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Purkinje-cell degeneration causes cerebellar ataxia; distinct from cardiac Purkinje conduction fibers.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-deepcerebellarnuclei"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Deep cerebellar nuclei" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Deep cerebellar nuclei"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry; Deep cerebellar nuclei is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">16</span><h3>Deep cerebellar nuclei</h3></div><small>Nuclei cerebelli</small><p>Dentate, interposed and fastigial groups deep in white matter.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Provide major cerebellar output to motor networks.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Lesions produce dysmetria and coordination deficits.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-superiorcerebellarpeduncle"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Superior cerebellar peduncle" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Superior cerebellar peduncle"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry; Superior cerebellar peduncle is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">17</span><h3>Superior cerebellar peduncle</h3></div><small>Pedunculus cerebellaris superior</small><p>Connects cerebellum to midbrain; many deep-nuclear outputs course here.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Transmits major cerebellar efferents.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Some afferent fibers also traverse it.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-middlecerebellarpeduncle"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Middle cerebellar peduncle" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="675" data-point-y="480" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Middle cerebellar peduncle"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry teaching plate: Middle cerebellar peduncle" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:67.5% 48.0%"></button><span class="number">18</span><h3>Middle cerebellar peduncle</h3></div><small>Pedunculus cerebellaris medius</small><p>Large connection from contralateral pontine nuclei to cerebellum.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Carries chiefly pontocerebellar afferents.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Links cerebral cortex via pons to cerebellum.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-inferiorcerebellarpeduncle"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Inferior cerebellar peduncle" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Inferior cerebellar peduncle"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry; Inferior cerebellar peduncle is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">19</span><h3>Inferior cerebellar peduncle</h3></div><small>Pedunculus cerebellaris inferior</small><p>Connects cerebellum to medulla/spinal pathways; mixed incoming and outgoing fibers.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Brings vestibular, spinal and olivary signals.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Do not assume all three peduncles carry the same direction.</p></div></section></div></section><section id="deep" class="content-section"><div class="eyebrow">Beyond the visible plate</div><h2>Deeper structures and tissue layers</h2><p class="section-lead">These 1 additional entries give anatomical context. They are deliberately not assigned a photographic pin on the regional image.</p><div class="part-list"><section class="part" id="part-detail_brainstemdeep_0"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" data-part-name="Nucleus tractus solitarii" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Nucleus tractus solitarii"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry; Nucleus tractus solitarii is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">20</span><h3>Nucleus tractus solitarii</h3></div><p>Dorsal medullary sensory nucleus receiving visceral and taste afferents.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Integrates cardiorespiratory reflex and taste pathways.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Medullary lesions disrupt autonomic reflexes.</p></div></section></div></section><section id="questions" class="content-section questions"><div class="eyebrow">Questions people ask</div><h2>Five questions about Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry</h2><p class="section-lead">Short answers grounded in the structures described above. Use the illustrated guide for anatomical context.</p><div class="qa-list"><div class="qa-item"><h3>What are the three parts of the brainstem?</h3><p>The brainstem consists of midbrain, pons and medulla. It carries major pathways and houses many cranial-nerve nuclei.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>What does the cerebellum do?</h3><p>The cerebellum helps coordinate movement, timing and balance by integrating sensory and motor information; it is not the same structure as the brainstem.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>What is the midbrain?</h3><p>Rostral brainstem around cerebral aqueduct between diencephalon and pons. Function: Hosts III/IV-related nuclei and visual/auditory orienting circuits.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>Where is the inferior colliculus located?</h3><p>Dorsal caudal midbrain auditory relay structure. Function: Integrates auditory pathway toward thalamus.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>What is the function of the cerebral peduncles?</h3><p>Function: Transmit corticospinal, corticobulbar and corticopontine signals. Ventral midbrain bundles carrying major descending cortical fibers.</p></div></div></section><section id="related" class="content-section related-section"><div class="eyebrow">Keep exploring</div><h2>Related anatomy guides</h2><p class="section-lead">Follow these connected structures and body systems in the illustrated atlas.</p><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="../brain/"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" width="500" height="500" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Brain" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Brain</strong><small>Midsagittal section: cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem and cerebellum around the ventricular system.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../cerebraldeep/"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways</strong><small>Trace cortex, white matter, corpus callosum, basal nuclei and thalamus on a realistic coronal section.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../meningescsf/"><img src="../../img/thumb/meninges-csf.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Meninges, CSF &amp; venous sinuses" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Meninges, CSF &amp; venous sinuses</strong><small>Follow dura, arachnoid and pia; then CSF from choroid plexus to subarachnoid drainage.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../cranialnerves/"><img src="../../img/thumb/cranial.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Cranial nerves" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Cranial nerves</strong><small>Twelve pairs leave the brain directly — smell to tongue movement — and they are examined in strict order in every neurological exam.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a></div></section><section id="sources" class="content-section sources"><div class="eyebrow">Reference desk</div><h2>Further reading</h2><p class="section-lead">General anatomy references and related source chapters for continued study. These links do not make the digital illustrations clinical images or certify every anatomical variant.</p><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK442010/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK10926/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538167/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608599/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ↗</a></li></ul><p class="disclaimer">This educational page is not medical advice and has not undergone formal clinical peer review. Spotted an error? <a href="../../about.html#contact">Contact the editor</a>.</p></section><div class="end-link"><div><strong>See the structures in context</strong><p>Open the realistic interactive plate, label lines, focus view and self-test.</p></div><a class="button" href="../../index.html#/brainstemdeep">Open interactive view →</a></div></main><aside class="sidebar" aria-label="On this page"><div class="side-card"><h2>In this guide</h2><a href="#overview">Overview</a><a href="#parts">Illustrated structures (19)</a><a href="#deep">Deep study notes (1)</a><a href="#questions">Five questions &amp; answers</a><a href="#related">Related guides</a><a href="#sources">Further reading</a></div><div class="side-card"><div class="count">20+</div><p>Documented anatomy and developmental entries in every Corpus chapter. Images are orientation aids, not clinical evidence.</p></div></aside></div><footer class="site-foot"><div class="shell foot-inner"><div><strong>Corpus Anatomy Atlas</strong><p>Illustrated anatomy for learning, not a diagnostic or surgical reference. Digital renders explain regions; they are not clinical photographs or substitutes for histology slides.</p></div><nav class="foot-links" aria-label="Footer"><a href="../../learn/">Study guides</a><a href="../../index.html">Interactive atlas</a><a href="../../about.html">About &amp; editorial policy</a><a href="../../privacy.html">Privacy</a></nav></div></footer><script defer src="../../reading.js?v=33"></script><script defer src="../../preview.js?v=48"></script></body></html>
