-<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="theme-color" content="#125e58"><title>Cerebral cortex and deep brain pathways | Corpus</title><meta name="description" content="Trace cortex, white matter, corpus callosum, basal nuclei and thalamus on a realistic coronal section."><meta name="robots" content="noindex,follow"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:title" content="Cerebral cortex and deep brain pathways"><meta property="og:description" content="Trace cortex, white matter, corpus callosum, basal nuclei and thalamus on a realistic coronal section."><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><link rel="icon" href="../../favicon.svg"><link rel="stylesheet" href="../../editorial.css?v=48"></head><body><a class="skip" href="#content">Skip to content</a><header class="site-head"><div class="shell head-inner"><a class="brand" href="../../index.html" aria-label="Corpus Anatomy Atlas home"><span class="brand-mark">♥</span><span class="brand-name">Corpus<small>Anatomy Atlas</small></span></a><nav class="head-nav" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="../../learn/" >Study guides</a><a href="../../index.html" >Interactive atlas</a><a href="../../index.html#/map" >Body map</a><a href="../../index.html#/quiz" >Quiz</a><a href="../../about.html" >About us</a></nav></div></header><div class="reading-track" aria-hidden="true"><span id="reading-progress"></span></div><nav class="crumb shell" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="../../index.html">Home</a><i>/</i><a href="../">Study guides</a><i>/</i><span>Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways</span></nav><section class="shell hero article-hero"><div class="eyebrow">Nervous · illustrated anatomy</div><h1>Cerebral cortex and deep brain pathways</h1><p>Trace cortex, white matter, corpus callosum, basal nuclei and thalamus on a realistic coronal section.</p><div class="action-row"><a class="button" href="../../index.html#/cerebraldeep">Open interactive atlas ↗</a><a class="button alt" href="#parts">Read the structures ↓</a></div><p class="byline">Corpus educational reference · 20 documented study entries · Updated 2026-10-18</p></section><div class="shell article-grid"><main id="content"><figure class="plate"><img src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Digital educational or regional anatomy illustration for Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways; " width="1408" height="768" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><figcaption>Regional teaching illustration.  Preview a part for a more relevant close-up when available; only identifiable structures receive a pointer.</figcaption></figure><section id="overview"><div class="eyebrow">Overview</div><h2>How this part of the body works</h2><p class="intro-text">Most cerebral neocortex has six microscopic layers, but cortical areas and older hippocampal cortex differ. Beneath cortical gray matter, association fibers link areas within a hemisphere, commissural fibers cross hemispheres and projection fibers connect cortex with deeper structures. The internal capsule carries densely packed motor and sensory pathways between thalamus/basal nuclei. The caudate and putamen form dorsal striatum; globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus and substantia nigra participate in motor circuits. The thalamus relays and modulates most sensory streams and important motor signals.</p><div class="facts"><div class="fact"><b>Body system</b><span>Nervous</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Location</b><span>Cerebral hemispheres: cortical ribbon, deep white matter, ventricles and diencephalon</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Blood supply</b><span>Anterior, middle and posterior cerebral arterial territories; perforators supply deep nuclei</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Innervation</b><span>Corticospinal, thalamocortical, commissural and association pathways</span></div></div></section><section id="parts" class="content-section"><div class="eyebrow">Illustrated structures</div><h2>Read the anatomy, part by part</h2><p class="section-lead">These 19 structures describe anatomy related to the chapter’s regional illustration. Each entry separates location, function and a clinical point.</p><div class="part-list"><section class="part" id="part-corticalgraymatter"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Cortical gray matter" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="290" data-point-y="260" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Cortical gray matter"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways teaching plate: Cortical gray matter" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:29.0% 26.0%"></button><span class="number">01</span><h3>Cortical gray matter</h3></div><small>Substantia grisea corticalis</small><p>Neuronal cell-body sheet folded into gyri and sulci.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Integrates sensory, motor and association information.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Regional cortical lesions give different deficits.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-layerimolecular"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Layer I · molecular" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Layer I · molecular"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways; Layer I · molecular is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">02</span><h3>Layer I · molecular</h3></div><small>Lamina molecularis</small><p>Superficial neuropil-rich layer below pia, with relatively few neuronal somata.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Supports horizontal intracortical connections.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Six-layer plan is a template, not identical in every area.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-layeriiexternalgranular"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Layer II · external granular" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Layer II · external granular"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways; Layer II · external granular is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">03</span><h3>Layer II · external granular</h3></div><small>Lamina granularis externa</small><p>Small pyramidal and granule-cell zone.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Contributes intracortical signaling.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Cell density varies across sensory and motor cortex.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-layeriiiexternalpyramidal"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Layer III · external pyramidal" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Layer III · external pyramidal"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways; Layer III · external pyramidal is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">04</span><h3>Layer III · external pyramidal</h3></div><small>Lamina pyramidalis externa</small><p>Pyramidal neurons send many corticocortical and callosal projections.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Links cortical regions and hemispheres.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>White-matter injury can disconnect otherwise intact cortex.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-layerivinternalgranular"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Layer IV · internal granular" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Layer IV · internal granular"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways; Layer IV · internal granular is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">05</span><h3>Layer IV · internal granular</h3></div><small>Lamina granularis interna</small><p>Prominent thalamic input layer, particularly in primary sensory cortex.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Receives major thalamocortical afferents.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Relatively thin in primary motor cortex.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-layervinternalpyramidal"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Layer V · internal pyramidal" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Layer V · internal pyramidal"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways; Layer V · internal pyramidal is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">06</span><h3>Layer V · internal pyramidal</h3></div><small>Lamina pyramidalis interna</small><p>Large pyramidal cells project to brainstem and spinal cord; Betz cells are an example.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Provides major descending cortical output.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Motor-pathway injury can cause contralateral weakness.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-layervimultiform"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Layer VI · multiform" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Layer VI · multiform"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways; Layer VI · multiform is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">07</span><h3>Layer VI · multiform</h3></div><small>Lamina multiformis</small><p>Deep layer adjoining white matter, with corticothalamic neurons.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Feeds back to thalamic circuits.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Not a standalone boundary visible in gross anatomy.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-subcorticalwhitematter"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Subcortical white matter" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="300" data-point-y="450" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Subcortical white matter"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways teaching plate: Subcortical white matter" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:30.0% 45.0%"></button><span class="number">08</span><h3>Subcortical white matter</h3></div><small>Substantia alba cerebri</small><p>Myelinated association, commissural and projection fibers beneath cortex.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Connects distributed gray-matter networks.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Small deep infarcts can interrupt multiple pathways.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-corpuscallosum"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Corpus callosum" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="500" data-point-y="385" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Corpus callosum"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways teaching plate: Corpus callosum" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 38.5%"></button><span class="number">09</span><h3>Corpus callosum</h3></div><p>Major interhemispheric white-matter bridge above lateral ventricles.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Coordinates homologous and heterologous cortical areas.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Callosal injury may cause disconnection syndromes.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-internalcapsule"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Internal capsule" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="590" data-point-y="540" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Internal capsule"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways teaching plate: Internal capsule" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:59.0% 54.0%"></button><span class="number">10</span><h3>Internal capsule</h3></div><small>Capsula interna</small><p>Compact fiber sheet between caudate/thalamus medially and lentiform nucleus laterally.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Carries ascending and descending cortical projections.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Small perforator stroke may produce dense deficits.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-caudatenucleus"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Caudate nucleus" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="425" data-point-y="410" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Caudate nucleus"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways teaching plate: Caudate nucleus" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:42.5% 41.0%"></button><span class="number">11</span><h3>Caudate nucleus</h3></div><small>Nucleus caudatus</small><p>C-shaped nucleus closely following lateral ventricle.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>With putamen forms dorsal striatum.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Striatal disease can alter movement and cognition.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-putamen"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Putamen" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="690" data-point-y="565" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Putamen"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways teaching plate: Putamen" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:69.0% 56.5%"></button><span class="number">12</span><h3>Putamen</h3></div><p>Lateral striatal nucleus adjacent to globus pallidus.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Receives cortical input for motor and associative loops.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Deep perforator disease may involve putamen.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-globuspallidus"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Globus pallidus" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Globus pallidus"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways; Globus pallidus is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">13</span><h3>Globus pallidus</h3></div><p>Medial lentiform nucleus divided into external and internal segments.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Internal segment contributes major basal-ganglia output.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Circuit imbalance affects initiation and suppression of movement.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-subthalamicnucleus"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Subthalamic nucleus" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Subthalamic nucleus"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways; Subthalamic nucleus is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">14</span><h3>Subthalamic nucleus</h3></div><small>Nucleus subthalamicus</small><p>Diencephalic nucleus below thalamus with excitatory projections toward pallidal output.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Modulates basal-ganglia movement circuit.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Focal lesion can cause contralateral hemiballismus.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-thalamus"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Thalamus" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="500" data-point-y="550" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Thalamus"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways teaching plate: Thalamus" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 55.0%"></button><span class="number">15</span><h3>Thalamus</h3></div><p>Paired deep gray-matter nuclei flanking third ventricle.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Routes selected sensory and motor signals toward cortex.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Olfaction is a notable exception to the usual initial sensory relay.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-hippocampalformation"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Hippocampal formation" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="355" data-point-y="765" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Hippocampal formation"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways teaching plate: Hippocampal formation" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:35.5% 76.5%"></button><span class="number">16</span><h3>Hippocampal formation</h3></div><small>Formatio hippocampi</small><p>Medial temporal folded cortex with fewer layers than typical neocortex.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Participates in memory encoding and spatial processing.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Bilateral injury can cause profound anterograde memory impairment.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-lateralventricle"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Lateral ventricle" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="500" data-point-y="410" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Lateral ventricle"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways teaching plate: Lateral ventricle" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 41.0%"></button><span class="number">17</span><h3>Lateral ventricle</h3></div><small>Ventriculus lateralis</small><p>Paired curved ventricular cavities beneath corpus callosum.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Conduct CSF toward foramina of Monro.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Its walls give landmarks for caudate and thalamus.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-primarymotorcortex"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Primary motor cortex" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Primary motor cortex"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways; Primary motor cortex is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">18</span><h3>Primary motor cortex</h3></div><small>Gyrus precentralis</small><p>Precentral gyrus of frontal lobe, largely Brodmann area 4.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Contributes voluntary movement commands.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Contralateral motor loss follows focal injury.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-primarysomatosensorycortex"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="Primary somatosensory cortex" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Primary somatosensory cortex"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways; Primary somatosensory cortex is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">19</span><h3>Primary somatosensory cortex</h3></div><small>Gyrus postcentralis</small><p>Postcentral gyrus of parietal lobe, chiefly areas 3, 1 and 2.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Processes body somatic sensory information.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Contralateral discriminative sensory deficits may occur.</p></div></section></div></section><section id="deep" class="content-section"><div class="eyebrow">Beyond the visible plate</div><h2>Deeper structures and tissue layers</h2><p class="section-lead">These 1 additional entries give anatomical context. They are deliberately not assigned a photographic pin on the regional image.</p><div class="part-list"><section class="part" id="part-detail_cerebraldeep_0"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/cerebral-deep.webp" data-part-name="External capsule" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for External capsule"><img src="../../img/thumb/cerebral-deep.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways; External capsule is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">20</span><h3>External capsule</h3></div><p>Thin white-matter sheet lateral to putamen and medial to claustrum.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Carries association and corticocortical fibers.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Distinct from the internal capsule near thalamus.</p></div></section></div></section><section id="questions" class="content-section questions"><div class="eyebrow">Questions people ask</div><h2>Five questions about Cerebrum · cortex &amp; deep pathways</h2><p class="section-lead">Short answers grounded in the structures described above. Use the illustrated guide for anatomical context.</p><div class="qa-list"><div class="qa-item"><h3>What lies beneath the cerebral cortex?</h3><p>Deep white-matter pathways connect cortical regions with each other and with subcortical structures, while deep gray nuclei and ventricles occupy related spaces.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>Does every part of the cerebral cortex have six layers?</h3><p>Most neocortex has six layers, but cortical regions vary, and older cortical areas such as hippocampal cortex have a different laminar organization.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>What is the cortical gray matter?</h3><p>Neuronal cell-body sheet folded into gyri and sulci. Function: Integrates sensory, motor and association information.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>Where is the layer II · external granular located?</h3><p>Small pyramidal and granule-cell zone. Function: Contributes intracortical signaling.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>What is the function of the layer IV · internal granular?</h3><p>Function: Receives major thalamocortical afferents. Prominent thalamic input layer, particularly in primary sensory cortex.</p></div></div></section><section id="related" class="content-section related-section"><div class="eyebrow">Keep exploring</div><h2>Related anatomy guides</h2><p class="section-lead">Follow these connected structures and body systems in the illustrated atlas.</p><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="../brain/"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" width="500" height="500" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Brain" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Brain</strong><small>Midsagittal section: cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem and cerebellum around the ventricular system.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../meningescsf/"><img src="../../img/thumb/meninges-csf.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Meninges, CSF &amp; venous sinuses" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Meninges, CSF &amp; venous sinuses</strong><small>Follow dura, arachnoid and pia; then CSF from choroid plexus to subarachnoid drainage.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../brainstemdeep/"><img src="../../img/thumb/brainstem-cerebellum.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Brainstem &amp; cerebellar circuitry</strong><small>Locate brainstem levels, cranial-nerve nuclei, pyramids and cerebellar peduncles, cortex and nuclei.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../spinalcord/"><img src="../../img/thumb/spinalcord.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Spinal cord" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Spinal cord</strong><small>Cross-section: a grey butterfly inside white tracts, wrapped in three meninges.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a></div></section><section id="sources" class="content-section sources"><div class="eyebrow">Reference desk</div><h2>Further reading</h2><p class="section-lead">General anatomy references and related source chapters for continued study. These links do not make the digital illustrations clinical images or certify every anatomical variant.</p><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK10889/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537247/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537141/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ↗</a></li></ul><p class="disclaimer">This educational page is not medical advice and has not undergone formal clinical peer review. Spotted an error? <a href="../../about.html#contact">Contact the editor</a>.</p></section><div class="end-link"><div><strong>See the structures in context</strong><p>Open the realistic interactive plate, label lines, focus view and self-test.</p></div><a class="button" href="../../index.html#/cerebraldeep">Open interactive view →</a></div></main><aside class="sidebar" aria-label="On this page"><div class="side-card"><h2>In this guide</h2><a href="#overview">Overview</a><a href="#parts">Illustrated structures (19)</a><a href="#deep">Deep study notes (1)</a><a href="#questions">Five questions &amp; answers</a><a href="#related">Related guides</a><a href="#sources">Further reading</a></div><div class="side-card"><div class="count">20+</div><p>Documented anatomy and developmental entries in every Corpus chapter. Images are orientation aids, not clinical evidence.</p></div></aside></div><footer class="site-foot"><div class="shell foot-inner"><div><strong>Corpus Anatomy Atlas</strong><p>Illustrated anatomy for learning, not a diagnostic or surgical reference. Digital renders explain regions; they are not clinical photographs or substitutes for histology slides.</p></div><nav class="foot-links" aria-label="Footer"><a href="../../learn/">Study guides</a><a href="../../index.html">Interactive atlas</a><a href="../../about.html">About &amp; editorial policy</a><a href="../../privacy.html">Privacy</a></nav></div></footer><script defer src="../../reading.js?v=33"></script><script defer src="../../preview.js?v=48"></script></body></html>
