+<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="theme-color" content="#125e58"><title>Zygote to blastocyst: cleavage, morula and hatching | Corpus</title><meta name="description" content="Explore early conceptus divisions, compaction, blastomeres, blastocoel, inner cell mass, trophoblast and hatching before implantation."><meta name="robots" content="noindex,follow"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:title" content="Zygote to blastocyst: cleavage, morula and hatching"><meta property="og:description" content="Explore early conceptus divisions, compaction, blastomeres, blastocoel, inner cell mass, trophoblast and hatching before implantation."><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><link rel="icon" href="../../favicon.svg"><link rel="stylesheet" href="../../editorial.css?v=48"></head><body><a class="skip" href="#content">Skip to content</a><header class="site-head"><div class="shell head-inner"><a class="brand" href="../../index.html" aria-label="Corpus Anatomy Atlas home"><span class="brand-mark">♥</span><span class="brand-name">Corpus<small>Anatomy Atlas</small></span></a><nav class="head-nav" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="../../learn/" >Study guides</a><a href="../../index.html" >Interactive atlas</a><a href="../../index.html#/map" >Body map</a><a href="../../index.html#/quiz" >Quiz</a><a href="../../about.html" >About us</a></nav></div></header><div class="reading-track" aria-hidden="true"><span id="reading-progress"></span></div><nav class="crumb shell" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="../../index.html">Home</a><i>/</i><a href="../">Study guides</a><i>/</i><span>Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions</span></nav><section class="shell hero article-hero"><div class="eyebrow">Conception to birth · illustrated anatomy</div><h1>Zygote to blastocyst: cleavage, morula and hatching</h1><p>Explore early conceptus divisions, compaction, blastomeres, blastocoel, inner cell mass, trophoblast and hatching before implantation.</p><div class="action-row"><a class="button" href="../../index.html#/cleavageblastocyst">Open interactive atlas ↗</a><a class="button alt" href="#parts">Read the structures ↓</a></div><p class="byline">Corpus educational reference · 20 documented study entries · Updated 2026-10-19</p></section><div class="shell article-grid"><main id="content"><figure class="plate"><img src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" alt="Digital educational or regional anatomy illustration for Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions; Original explanatory vector-style cell diagram, not a microscopic photograph." width="1264" height="689" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><figcaption>Regional teaching illustration. Original explanatory vector-style cell diagram, not a microscopic photograph. Preview a part for a more relevant close-up when available; only identifiable structures receive a pointer.</figcaption></figure><section id="overview"><div class="eyebrow">Overview</div><h2>How this part of the body works</h2><p class="intro-text">After fertilization in a uterine tube, the zygote divides into blastomeres while moving toward the uterus. Early cleavage increases cell number without major increase in overall size inside the zona pellucida. Compaction forms a morula, then a fluid-filled blastocoel and two broad cell populations define the blastocyst: outer trophectoderm/trophoblast and an inner cell mass. Hatching from the zona precedes adhesion to endometrium. Timing varies; the illustration shows one blastocyst only, not a timed sequence or an implanted embryo.</p><div class="facts"><div class="fact"><b>Body system</b><span>Conception to birth</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Location</b><span>Early conceptus moves from tube toward uterine cavity</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Blood supply</b><span>Depends on testicular, ovarian, uterine and placental stage; see the named entries</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Innervation</b><span>Maternal pelvic autonomic and somatic pathways vary by structure; gametes have no nerve supply</span></div></div></section><section id="under-the-lens" class="content-section lens-reading"><div class="eyebrow">Microscopic-scale anatomy</div><h2>From organ to tissue</h2><p class="section-lead">These are educational cutaways, not literal magnifications of an organ pixel or laboratory slides. A ring marks the approximate tissue region; part-specific lines appear only where identifiable.</p><div class="lens-tiles"><article class="lens-tile"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger lens-tile-trigger" data-plate-preview data-part-name="Zygote" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="420" data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-zygote-diagram.webp" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-lens-title="Zygote · first cleavage plan" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-1-fertilization" aria-label="Enlarge organ-to-lens view of Zygote"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-zygote-diagram.webp" width="450" height="253" alt="Representative tissue cutaway for Zygote" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></button><div class="lens-tile-copy"><small>ORGAN → UNDER THE LENS</small><h3>Zygote · first cleavage plan</h3><p>Tap to compare the organ region with a tissue-level teaching illustration.</p><a href="#part-zygote">Read about Zygote ↓</a></div></article><article class="lens-tile"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger lens-tile-trigger" data-plate-preview data-part-name="First cleavage" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="420" data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-two-cell-diagram.webp" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-lens-title="Two-cell stage cutaway" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" aria-label="Enlarge organ-to-lens view of First cleavage"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-two-cell-diagram.webp" width="450" height="253" alt="Representative tissue cutaway for First cleavage" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></button><div class="lens-tile-copy"><small>ORGAN → UNDER THE LENS</small><h3>Two-cell stage cutaway</h3><p>Tap to compare the organ region with a tissue-level teaching illustration.</p><a href="#part-firstdivision">Read about First cleavage ↓</a></div></article><article class="lens-tile"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger lens-tile-trigger" data-plate-preview data-part-name="Four-cell stage" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="420" data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-four-cell-diagram.webp" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-lens-title="Four-cell stage cutaway" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" aria-label="Enlarge organ-to-lens view of Four-cell stage"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-four-cell-diagram.webp" width="450" height="253" alt="Representative tissue cutaway for Four-cell stage" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></button><div class="lens-tile-copy"><small>ORGAN → UNDER THE LENS</small><h3>Four-cell stage cutaway</h3><p>Tap to compare the organ region with a tissue-level teaching illustration.</p><a href="#part-fourcell">Read about Four-cell stage ↓</a></div></article><article class="lens-tile"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger lens-tile-trigger" data-plate-preview data-part-name="Eight-cell stage" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="420" data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-eight-cell-diagram.webp" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-lens-title="Eight-cell stage cutaway" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" aria-label="Enlarge organ-to-lens view of Eight-cell stage"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-eight-cell-diagram.webp" width="450" height="253" alt="Representative tissue cutaway for Eight-cell stage" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></button><div class="lens-tile-copy"><small>ORGAN → UNDER THE LENS</small><h3>Eight-cell stage cutaway</h3><p>Tap to compare the organ region with a tissue-level teaching illustration.</p><a href="#part-eightcell">Read about Eight-cell stage ↓</a></div></article><article class="lens-tile"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger lens-tile-trigger" data-plate-preview data-part-name="Compaction" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="420" data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-morula-diagram.webp" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-lens-title="Morula cutaway" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" aria-label="Enlarge organ-to-lens view of Compaction"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-morula-diagram.webp" width="450" height="253" alt="Representative tissue cutaway for Compaction" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></button><div class="lens-tile-copy"><small>ORGAN → UNDER THE LENS</small><h3>Morula cutaway</h3><p>Tap to compare the organ region with a tissue-level teaching illustration.</p><a href="#part-compaction">Read about Compaction ↓</a></div></article></div></section><section id="repro-path" class="content-section repro-reading"><div class="eyebrow">Gametes to birth · separate chapters</div><h2>Follow every stage independently</h2><p class="section-lead">Sperm and egg are separate branches before fertilization. Obstetric gestational weeks are usually counted from the last menstrual period, roughly two weeks before conception. Digital labor-stage illustrations model separate moments, not clinical birth photographs or patient measurements.</p><nav class="repro-timeline" aria-label="Conception and childbirth study pathway"><a class="repro-stage " href="../spermanatomy/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-sperm.webp" width="450" height="251" alt="Regional educational context for Sperm cell" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>01 / 11</small><strong>Sperm cell</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../oocyteanatomy/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-oocyte.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Regional educational context for Egg / oocyte" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>02 / 11</small><strong>Egg / oocyte</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../fertilization/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-fertilization.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Regional educational context for Fertilization" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>03 / 11</small><strong>Fertilization</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage selected" href="../cleavageblastocyst/" aria-current="page"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Regional educational context for Early divisions" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>04 / 11</small><strong>Early divisions</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../implantation/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-implantation.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Regional educational context for Implantation" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>05 / 11</small><strong>Implantation</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../placentaanatomy/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-placenta.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Regional educational context for Placenta" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>06 / 11</small><strong>Placenta</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../embryodevelopment/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-embryo.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Regional educational context for Embryo" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>07 / 11</small><strong>Embryo</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../fetaldevelopment/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-fetus-second-trimester.webp" width="450" height="251" alt="Regional educational context for Fetus" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>08 / 11</small><strong>Fetus</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../laborfirststage/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-labor-stage-one.webp" width="450" height="251" alt="Regional educational context for Labor 1 · cervix" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>09 / 11</small><strong>Labor 1 · cervix</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../laborsecondstage/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-labor-stage-two.webp" width="450" height="251" alt="Regional educational context for Labor 2 · baby" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>10 / 11</small><strong>Labor 2 · baby</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../laborthirdstage/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-labor-stage-three.webp" width="450" height="251" alt="Regional educational context for Labor 3 · placenta" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>11 / 11</small><strong>Labor 3 · placenta</strong></span></a></nav></section><section id="repro-gallery" class="content-section repro-gallery"><div class="eyebrow">Stage-specific picture library</div><h2>See the anatomy at the right scale</h2><p class="section-lead">Tap an image to enlarge and tap again to close. These are digital educational models, not microscope photos, measured ultrasounds, clinical birth images or exact developmental clocks.</p><div class="repro-gallery-grid"><figure class="repro-gallery-card"><button type="button" class="repro-gallery-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-zygote-diagram.webp" data-part-name="One-cell zygote" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" data-preview-note="Original explanatory vector-style cell diagram, not a microscopic photograph." aria-label="Enlarge One-cell zygote"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-zygote-diagram.webp" width="450" height="253" alt="Digital educational illustration: One-cell zygote" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span aria-hidden="true">⤢</span></button><figcaption><small>01 / 6</small><strong>One-cell zygote</strong><p>Original explanatory vector-style cell diagram, not a microscopic photograph.</p></figcaption></figure><figure class="repro-gallery-card"><button type="button" class="repro-gallery-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-two-cell-diagram.webp" data-part-name="Two-cell cleavage" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" data-preview-note="Two blastomeres within a conceptual zona; relative positions are simplified." aria-label="Enlarge Two-cell cleavage"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-two-cell-diagram.webp" width="450" height="253" alt="Digital educational illustration: Two-cell cleavage" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span aria-hidden="true">⤢</span></button><figcaption><small>02 / 6</small><strong>Two-cell cleavage</strong><p>Two blastomeres within a conceptual zona; relative positions are simplified.</p></figcaption></figure><figure class="repro-gallery-card"><button type="button" class="repro-gallery-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-four-cell-diagram.webp" data-part-name="Four-cell cleavage" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" data-preview-note="Four blastomeres inside the zona; cell divisions do not imply marked growth of the whole conceptus." aria-label="Enlarge Four-cell cleavage"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-four-cell-diagram.webp" width="450" height="253" alt="Digital educational illustration: Four-cell cleavage" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span aria-hidden="true">⤢</span></button><figcaption><small>03 / 6</small><strong>Four-cell cleavage</strong><p>Four blastomeres inside the zona; cell divisions do not imply marked growth of the whole conceptus.</p></figcaption></figure><figure class="repro-gallery-card"><button type="button" class="repro-gallery-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-eight-cell-diagram.webp" data-part-name="Eight-cell cleavage" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" data-preview-note="Eight conceptual blastomeres remain within the zona; this is a vector-style diagram, not a micrograph." aria-label="Enlarge Eight-cell cleavage"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-eight-cell-diagram.webp" width="450" height="253" alt="Digital educational illustration: Eight-cell cleavage" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span aria-hidden="true">⤢</span></button><figcaption><small>04 / 6</small><strong>Eight-cell cleavage</strong><p>Eight conceptual blastomeres remain within the zona; this is a vector-style diagram, not a micrograph.</p></figcaption></figure><figure class="repro-gallery-card"><button type="button" class="repro-gallery-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-morula-diagram.webp" data-part-name="Compact morula" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" data-preview-note="A solid group of 16 conceptual cells; no blastocoel yet." aria-label="Enlarge Compact morula"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-morula-diagram.webp" width="450" height="253" alt="Digital educational illustration: Compact morula" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span aria-hidden="true">⤢</span></button><figcaption><small>05 / 6</small><strong>Compact morula</strong><p>A solid group of 16 conceptual cells; no blastocoel yet.</p></figcaption></figure><figure class="repro-gallery-card"><button type="button" class="repro-gallery-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-part-name="Blastocyst · fluid cavity and cell mass" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" data-preview-note="Different later stage: a hollow blastocyst, not a zygote or morula." aria-label="Enlarge Blastocyst · fluid cavity and cell mass"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Digital educational illustration: Blastocyst · fluid cavity and cell mass" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span aria-hidden="true">⤢</span></button><figcaption><small>06 / 6</small><strong>Blastocyst · fluid cavity and cell mass</strong><p>Different later stage: a hollow blastocyst, not a zygote or morula.</p></figcaption></figure></div></section><section id="parts" class="content-section"><div class="eyebrow">Beyond the visible plate</div><h2>Stage anatomy and developmental milestones</h2><p class="section-lead">These 20 anatomy and development entries explain each stage without placing false pointers on an illustrative context plate.</p><div class="part-list"><section class="part" id="part-zygote"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-zygote-diagram.webp" data-part-name="Zygote" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-lens-title="Zygote · first cleavage plan" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-1-fertilization" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="420" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Zygote"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-zygote-diagram.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Zygote; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">01</span><h3>Zygote</h3></div><p>Diploid one-cell conceptus results after fertilization of one oocyte by one sperm.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Begins early development.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A zygote has not yet implanted and is not a fetus.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Original explanatory vector-style cell diagram, not a microscopic photograph.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-firstdivision"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-two-cell-diagram.webp" data-part-name="First cleavage" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-lens-title="Two-cell stage cutaway" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="420" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for First cleavage"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-two-cell-diagram.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for First cleavage; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">02</span><h3>First cleavage</h3></div><p>The zygote divides into two blastomeres without the usual cell growth between divisions.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Begins increase in cell number.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Exact day of the first division is variable.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Two blastomeres within a conceptual zona; relative positions are simplified.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-blastomere"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-two-cell-diagram.webp" data-part-name="Blastomere" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-lens-title="Two-cell stage cutaway" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="420" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Blastomere"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-two-cell-diagram.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Blastomere; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">03</span><h3>Blastomere</h3></div><p>Each early daughter cell forms as the conceptus cleaves in its surrounding zona.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Builds the multicellular early conceptus.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>One blastomere alone is not the same as a mature embryo.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Two blastomeres within a conceptual zona; relative positions are simplified.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-fourcell"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-four-cell-diagram.webp" data-part-name="Four-cell stage" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-lens-title="Four-cell stage cutaway" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="420" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Four-cell stage"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-four-cell-diagram.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Four-cell stage; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">04</span><h3>Four-cell stage</h3></div><p>Additional cleavage creates a small group of blastomeres still enclosed by zona.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Extends early proliferation.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>This single blastocyst plate does not show a four-cell stage.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Four blastomeres inside the zona; cell divisions do not imply marked growth of the whole conceptus.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-eightcell"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-eight-cell-diagram.webp" data-part-name="Eight-cell stage" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-lens-title="Eight-cell stage cutaway" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="420" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Eight-cell stage"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-eight-cell-diagram.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Eight-cell stage; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">05</span><h3>Eight-cell stage</h3></div><p>Further divisions make smaller blastomeres as the early conceptus approaches compaction.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Sets up more extensive cell-to-cell contact.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Do not count blastocyst cells to infer a precise day.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Eight conceptual blastomeres remain within the zona; this is a vector-style diagram, not a micrograph.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-zonaretained"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-part-name="Zona pellucida during transit" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Zona pellucida during transit"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions; Zona pellucida during transit is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">06</span><h3>Zona pellucida during transit</h3></div><p>Extracellular glycoprotein envelope persists around early cleavage stages.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Limits premature attachment during tubal passage.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A tube is not the intended implantation site.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Different later stage: a hollow blastocyst, not a zygote or morula.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-transport"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/gyn-adnexa.webp" data-part-name="Conceptus tubal transport" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Conceptus tubal transport"><img src="../../img/thumb/gyn-adnexa.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions; Conceptus tubal transport is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">07</span><h3>Conceptus tubal transport</h3></div><p>Cilia and tubal contractile activity help move the dividing conceptus into uterine cavity.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Positions conceptus for later endometrial interaction.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Impaired transport may increase ectopic pregnancy risk.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> A related regional anatomy model; the individual event or cell is not separately identifiable in this picture.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-compaction"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-morula-diagram.webp" data-part-name="Compaction" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-lens-title="Morula cutaway" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="420" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Compaction"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-morula-diagram.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Compaction; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">08</span><h3>Compaction</h3></div><p>Blastomeres strengthen contacts and organize an outer and inner cell arrangement.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Prepares for tissue-population divergence.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Not individually resolved in a late blastocyst teaching render.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> A solid group of 16 conceptual cells; no blastocoel yet.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-morula"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-morula-diagram.webp" data-part-name="Morula" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-lens-title="Morula cutaway" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="420" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Morula"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-morula-diagram.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Morula; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">09</span><h3>Morula</h3></div><p>Solid compact cluster of blastomeres forms before a fluid-filled cavity develops.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Transitional stage before blastocyst formation.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A morula is not a hollow blastocyst.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> A solid group of 16 conceptual cells; no blastocoel yet.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-cavitation"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-part-name="Cavitation" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Cavitation"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions; Cavitation is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">10</span><h3>Cavitation</h3></div><p>Fluid accumulates within cell cluster and a blastocoel begins to form.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Establishes fluid-filled inner space.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>The large cavity in the plate depicts a blastocyst, not a fetus.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Different later stage: a hollow blastocyst, not a zygote or morula.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-blastocoel"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-part-name="Blastocoel" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Blastocoel"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions; Blastocoel is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">11</span><h3>Blastocoel</h3></div><p>Fluid-filled cavity occupies much of a blastocyst.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Separates inner cell group and outer cell layer.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Not the later amniotic sac around a fetus.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Different later stage: a hollow blastocyst, not a zygote or morula.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-trophectoderm"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-part-name="Outer trophoblast layer" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Outer trophoblast layer"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions; Outer trophoblast layer is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">12</span><h3>Outer trophoblast layer</h3></div><p>Outer cell population surrounds the blastocyst; later contributes to extraembryonic tissues.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Interacts with maternal endometrium during implantation.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A single outer-cell layer does not equal a mature placenta.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Different later stage: a hollow blastocyst, not a zygote or morula.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-innercell"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-part-name="Inner cell mass" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Inner cell mass"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions; Inner cell mass is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">13</span><h3>Inner cell mass</h3></div><p>Localized cluster on one side of blastocyst contributes to embryo proper and some extraembryonic tissues.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Seeds later embryonic development.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>It is a group of cells, not a miniature baby.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Different later stage: a hollow blastocyst, not a zygote or morula.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-pole"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-part-name="Embryonic pole" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Embryonic pole"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions; Embryonic pole is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">14</span><h3>Embryonic pole</h3></div><p>Side of the blastocyst containing the inner cell mass.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Defines a directional relationship as implantation begins.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>This is not the maternal cervix or a fetal head.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Different later stage: a hollow blastocyst, not a zygote or morula.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-hatching"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-part-name="Hatching from zona" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Hatching from zona"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions; Hatching from zona is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">15</span><h3>Hatching from zona</h3></div><p>Expanding blastocyst emerges from the zona pellucida.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Allows cell surface to attach to receptive endometrium.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>An unhatched conceptus has not directly attached.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Different later stage: a hollow blastocyst, not a zygote or morula.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-uterineentry"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/gyn-uterus.webp" data-part-name="Entry into uterine cavity" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Entry into uterine cavity"><img src="../../img/thumb/gyn-uterus.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions; Entry into uterine cavity is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">16</span><h3>Entry into uterine cavity</h3></div><p>Preimplantation conceptus reaches endometrial environment after tubal transit.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Provides setting for hatching and eventual attachment.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Being in uterus is not the same as implantation.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> A related regional anatomy model; the individual event or cell is not separately identifiable in this picture.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-secretions"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/gyn-uterus.webp" data-part-name="Endometrial secretions" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Endometrial secretions"><img src="../../img/thumb/gyn-uterus.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions; Endometrial secretions is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">17</span><h3>Endometrial secretions</h3></div><p>Uterine gland fluid supplies early trophic support before placental circulation is established.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Supports preimplantation growth.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>No mature placenta feeds the early blastocyst on this image.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> A related regional anatomy model; the individual event or cell is not separately identifiable in this picture.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-variability"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-zygote-diagram.webp" data-part-name="Variable early timing" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Variable early timing"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-zygote-diagram.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions; Variable early timing is not separately marked" width="450" height="253" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">18</span><h3>Variable early timing</h3></div><p>Division, hatching and attachment timing vary from conceptus to conceptus.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Prevents treating image as a clinical day-by-day clock.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Pregnancy dating uses clinical methods rather than counting drawn cells.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Original explanatory vector-style cell diagram, not a microscopic photograph.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-twins"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-part-name="Early embryonic splitting" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Early embryonic splitting"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions; Early embryonic splitting is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">19</span><h3>Early embryonic splitting</h3></div><p>One early conceptus can split and contribute to monozygotic twins.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Explains a distinct origin from two separately fertilized oocytes.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>The plate depicts only one blastocyst.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Different later stage: a hollow blastocyst, not a zygote or morula.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-transition"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-part-name="Preimplantation to implantation" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Preimplantation to implantation"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions; Preimplantation to implantation is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">20</span><h3>Preimplantation to implantation</h3></div><p>After hatching, trophoblast can adhere to receptive uterine lining.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Links this chapter to the separate implantation chapter.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Do not show a blastocyst free in lumen as already embedded.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Different later stage: a hollow blastocyst, not a zygote or morula.</p></div></section></div></section><section id="questions" class="content-section questions"><div class="eyebrow">Questions people ask</div><h2>Five questions about Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions</h2><p class="section-lead">Short answers grounded in the structures described above. Use the illustrated guide for anatomical context.</p><div class="qa-list"><div class="qa-item"><h3>What does cleavage do to a newly formed zygote?</h3><p>It produces more, initially smaller, blastomeres while the conceptus remains within the zona pellucida. Early divisions do not require the whole sphere to grow much larger at every division.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>How is a morula different from a blastocyst?</h3><p>A morula is a compact solid cluster. A blastocyst has a fluid-filled blastocoel, an outer cell layer and a localized inner cell mass; the illustration depicts the latter, not every earlier stage.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>What does the inner cell mass become?</h3><p>Its descendants contribute to the embryo proper and certain extraembryonic structures. A blastocyst cell cluster is not a miniature fully formed fetus.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>Why does the blastocyst hatch from the zona pellucida?</h3><p>Loss of the extracellular zona permits trophoblast contact with receptive uterine endometrium. A conceptus still enclosed by the zona is not already embedded in uterine tissue.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>Does a blastocyst in the uterus mean pregnancy has implanted?</h3><p>Not necessarily. Arrival in the uterine cavity and hatching precede attachment to endometrium; implantation is a separate stage with variable timing.</p></div></div></section><section id="related" class="content-section related-section"><div class="eyebrow">Keep exploring</div><h2>Related anatomy guides</h2><p class="section-lead">Follow these connected structures and body systems in the illustrated atlas.</p><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="../fertilization/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-fertilization.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Fertilization · sperm meets oocyte" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Fertilization · sperm meets oocyte</strong><small>Follow sperm transit, capacitation, zona interaction, membrane fusion and formation of the diploid zygote.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../implantation/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-implantation.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium</strong><small>Separate implantation stage: endometrial receptivity, hatching, trophoblast invasion and early hCG.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../gynuterus/"><img src="../../img/thumb/gyn-uterus.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Uterus — layers &amp; cavity" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Uterus — layers &amp; cavity</strong><small>The womb in cross-section: endometrium, junctional zone, myometrium, cavity, isthmus and the key clinical boundaries.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../femalepelvis/"><img src="../../img/thumb/female-pelvis.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Female pelvis &amp; perineum" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Female pelvis &amp; perineum</strong><small>The female pelvic floor in sagittal section: bladder and urethra, vagina and anteverted uterus, rectum behind — plus the ligaments and pouches that position them.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a></div></section><section id="sources" class="content-section sources"><div class="eyebrow">Reference desk</div><h2>Further reading</h2><p class="section-lead">General anatomy references and related source chapters for continued study. These links do not make the digital illustrations clinical images or certify every anatomical variant.</p><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/pediatrics/perinatal-problems/gestational-age" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">msdmanuals.com ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-1-fertilization" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li></ul><p class="disclaimer">This educational page is not medical advice and has not undergone formal clinical peer review. Spotted an error? <a href="../../about.html#contact">Contact the editor</a>.</p></section><div class="end-link"><div><strong>See the structures in context</strong><p>Open the regional digital plate, study entries and a short self-test. Visible markers occur only for identifiable sperm and oocyte structures.</p></div><a class="button" href="../../index.html#/cleavageblastocyst">Open interactive view →</a></div></main><aside class="sidebar" aria-label="On this page"><div class="side-card"><h2>In this guide</h2><a href="#overview">Overview</a><a href="#parts">Study entries (20)</a><a href="#under-the-lens">Under the lens</a><a href="#repro-path">Conception to birth path</a><a href="#repro-gallery">Picture library</a><a href="#questions">Five questions &amp; answers</a><a href="#related">Related guides</a><a href="#sources">Further reading</a></div><div class="side-card"><div class="count">20+</div><p>Documented anatomy and developmental entries in every Corpus chapter. Images are orientation aids, not clinical evidence.</p></div></aside></div><footer class="site-foot"><div class="shell foot-inner"><div><strong>Corpus Anatomy Atlas</strong><p>Illustrated anatomy for learning, not a diagnostic or surgical reference. Digital renders explain regions; they are not clinical photographs or substitutes for histology slides.</p></div><nav class="foot-links" aria-label="Footer"><a href="../../learn/">Study guides</a><a href="../../index.html">Interactive atlas</a><a href="../../about.html">About &amp; editorial policy</a><a href="../../privacy.html">Privacy</a></nav></div></footer><script defer src="../../reading.js?v=33"></script><script defer src="../../preview.js?v=48"></script></body></html>
