-<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="theme-color" content="#125e58"><title>Heart anatomy: chambers, valves and blood flow | Corpus</title><meta name="description" content="A four-chambered muscular pump with four valves and its own electrical conduction system."><meta name="robots" content="noindex,follow"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:title" content="Heart anatomy: chambers, valves and blood flow"><meta property="og:description" content="A four-chambered muscular pump with four valves and its own electrical conduction system."><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><link rel="icon" href="../../favicon.svg"><link rel="stylesheet" href="../../editorial.css?v=48"></head><body><a class="skip" href="#content">Skip to content</a><header class="site-head"><div class="shell head-inner"><a class="brand" href="../../index.html" aria-label="Corpus Anatomy Atlas home"><span class="brand-mark">♥</span><span class="brand-name">Corpus<small>Anatomy Atlas</small></span></a><nav class="head-nav" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="../../learn/" >Study guides</a><a href="../../index.html" >Interactive atlas</a><a href="../../index.html#/map" >Body map</a><a href="../../index.html#/quiz" >Quiz</a><a href="../../about.html" >About us</a></nav></div></header><div class="reading-track" aria-hidden="true"><span id="reading-progress"></span></div><nav class="crumb shell" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="../../index.html">Home</a><i>/</i><a href="../">Study guides</a><i>/</i><span>Heart</span></nav><section class="shell hero article-hero"><div class="eyebrow">Circulatory · illustrated anatomy</div><h1>Heart anatomy: chambers, valves and blood flow</h1><p>A four-chambered muscular pump with four valves and its own electrical conduction system.</p><div class="action-row"><a class="button" href="../../index.html#/heart">Open interactive atlas ↗</a><a class="button alt" href="#parts">Read the structures ↓</a></div><p class="byline">Corpus educational reference · 22 documented study entries · Updated 2026-10-19</p></section><div class="shell article-grid"><main id="content"><figure class="plate"><img src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" alt="Digital educational or regional anatomy illustration for Heart; " width="1200" height="1063" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><figcaption>Regional teaching illustration.  Preview a part for a more relevant close-up when available; only identifiable structures receive a pointer.</figcaption></figure><section id="overview"><div class="eyebrow">Overview</div><h2>How this part of the body works</h2><p class="intro-text">The heart is a double pump. Its right side receives deoxygenated blood from the venae cavae and sends it to the lungs; its left side receives oxygenated blood from the pulmonary veins and ejects it into the aorta. The two sides never mix because of the interatrial and interventricular septa, and one-way valves keep blood moving forwards. Contraction is triggered by an electrical impulse that begins in the sinoatrial node and sweeps through the atria, the atrioventricular node, the bundle of His and the Purkinje fibres.</p><div class="facts"><div class="fact"><b>Body system</b><span>Circulatory</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Location</b><span>Middle mediastinum, behind the sternum</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Blood supply</b><span>Right &amp; left coronary arteries (first branches of the aorta)</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Innervation</b><span>Cardiac plexus — vagus (slows) + sympathetic T1–T4 (speeds up)</span></div></div></section><section id="parts" class="content-section"><div class="eyebrow">Illustrated structures</div><h2>Read the anatomy, part by part</h2><p class="section-lead">These 22 structures describe anatomy related to the chapter’s regional illustration. Each entry separates location, function and a clinical point.</p><div class="part-list"><section class="part" id="part-svc"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Superior vena cava" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Superior vena cava"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Heart; Superior vena cava is not separately marked" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">01</span><h3>Superior vena cava</h3></div><small>Vena cava superior</small><p>The large vein draining the head, neck, upper limbs and thorax. It enters the upper part of the right atrium.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Returns deoxygenated blood from everything above the diaphragm.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Obstruction (SVC syndrome) causes facial and arm swelling with distended neck veins.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-ivc"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Inferior vena cava" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Inferior vena cava"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Heart; Inferior vena cava is not separately marked" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">02</span><h3>Inferior vena cava</h3></div><small>Vena cava inferior</small><p>The largest vein in the body. It collects blood from the abdomen, pelvis and lower limbs and enters the inferior part of the right atrium.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Returns deoxygenated blood from below the diaphragm.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Compression in late pregnancy reduces venous return and can cause supine hypotension.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-aorta"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Aorta" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="583" data-point-y="84" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Aorta"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Heart teaching plate: Aorta" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:58.3% 8.4%"></button><span class="number">03</span><h3>Aorta</h3></div><p>The largest artery in the body. It arises from the left ventricle, arches over the left main bronchus and descends through the thorax and abdomen.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Distributes oxygenated blood to the entire systemic circulation.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A tear in the wall (aortic dissection) causes sudden tearing chest or back pain — a surgical emergency.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-pta"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Pulmonary trunk" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="500" data-point-y="126" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Pulmonary trunk"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Heart teaching plate: Pulmonary trunk" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 12.6%"></button><span class="number">04</span><h3>Pulmonary trunk</h3></div><small>Truncus pulmonalis</small><p>Leaves the right ventricle and divides into right and left pulmonary arteries. Uniquely, it carries deoxygenated blood.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Carries blood from the right ventricle to the lungs for oxygenation.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A clot lodging here (pulmonary embolism) causes breathlessness, pleuritic pain and hypoxia.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-pv"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Pulmonary veins" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Pulmonary veins"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Heart; Pulmonary veins is not separately marked" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">05</span><h3>Pulmonary veins</h3></div><small>Venae pulmonales</small><p>Four veins — two from each lung — that carry freshly oxygenated blood into the left atrium.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Return oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Ectopic electrical foci in the pulmonary vein ostia are the commonest trigger of atrial fibrillation.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-myo"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Myocardium" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="733" data-point-y="800" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Myocardium"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Heart teaching plate: Myocardium" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:73.3% 80.0%"></button><span class="number">06</span><h3>Myocardium</h3></div><p>The thick middle layer of the heart wall, made of branching, interlinked cardiac muscle fibres joined by intercalated discs.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Contracts to generate the pressure that ejects blood.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Death of myocardium from a blocked coronary artery is a myocardial infarction; the dead tissue scars rather than regrows.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-ra"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Right atrium" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="150" data-point-y="368" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Right atrium"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Heart teaching plate: Right atrium" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:15.0% 36.8%"></button><span class="number">07</span><h3>Right atrium</h3></div><small>Atrium dextrum</small><p>Thin-walled receiving chamber for the superior and inferior venae cavae and the coronary sinus. Contains the fossa ovalis and the right auricle.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Collects deoxygenated blood and passes it to the right ventricle through the tricuspid valve.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Houses the SA node, so it initiates every normal heartbeat.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-la"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Left atrium" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Left atrium"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Heart; Left atrium is not separately marked" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">08</span><h3>Left atrium</h3></div><small>Atrium sinistrum</small><p>The most posterior chamber, receiving the four pulmonary veins.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Passes oxygenated blood to the left ventricle through the mitral valve.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Enlargement can press on the oesophagus (dysphagia) or the recurrent laryngeal nerve (hoarseness).</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-rv"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Right ventricle" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="208" data-point-y="505" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Right ventricle"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Heart teaching plate: Right ventricle" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:20.8% 50.5%"></button><span class="number">09</span><h3>Right ventricle</h3></div><small>Ventriculus dexter</small><p>Crescent-shaped chamber with a thinner wall than the left, lined by trabeculae carneae and containing the moderator band.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Pumps deoxygenated blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Fails first in pulmonary hypertension (cor pulmonale), causing raised jugular venous pressure and ankle oedema.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-lv"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Left ventricle" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="667" data-point-y="684" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Left ventricle"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Heart teaching plate: Left ventricle" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:66.7% 68.4%"></button><span class="number">10</span><h3>Left ventricle</h3></div><small>Ventriculus sinister</small><p>The thickest-walled chamber — roughly three times the right ventricle — because it must drive blood around the whole body.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Ejects oxygenated blood through the aortic valve into the aorta.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Pressure overload (hypertension, aortic stenosis) causes concentric hypertrophy and later heart failure.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-ias"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Interatrial septum" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Interatrial septum"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Heart; Interatrial septum is not separately marked" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">11</span><h3>Interatrial septum</h3></div><small>Septum interatriale</small><p>The thin wall between the two atria. Its shallow depression, the fossa ovalis, marks where the foramen ovale closed after birth.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Keeps the atrial circulations separate.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A patent foramen ovale persists in ~25% of adults and can allow a paradoxical embolus to cause a stroke.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-ivs"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Interventricular septum" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="517" data-point-y="652" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Interventricular septum"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Heart teaching plate: Interventricular septum" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:51.7% 65.2%"></button><span class="number">12</span><h3>Interventricular septum</h3></div><small>Septum interventriculare</small><p>Thick muscular wall between the ventricles, with a thin membranous part just below the aortic valve.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Prevents mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood and carries the bundle branches.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>The membranous part is the commonest site of a ventricular septal defect, the most frequent congenital heart lesion.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-tricuspid"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Tricuspid valve" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="208" data-point-y="316" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Tricuspid valve"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Heart teaching plate: Tricuspid valve" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:20.8% 31.6%"></button><span class="number">13</span><h3>Tricuspid valve</h3></div><small>Valva tricuspidalis</small><p>Three cusps (anterior, posterior, septal) between right atrium and right ventricle.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Closes at ventricular systole to stop backflow into the right atrium.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Regurgitation produces large &quot;v&quot; waves in the jugular venous pulse and a pulsatile liver.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-mitral"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Mitral valve" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="633" data-point-y="316" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Mitral valve"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Heart teaching plate: Mitral valve" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:63.3% 31.6%"></button><span class="number">14</span><h3>Mitral valve</h3></div><small>Valva mitralis</small><p>The bicuspid valve between left atrium and left ventricle, with anterior and posterior cusps.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Closes at ventricular systole to stop backflow into the left atrium.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Rheumatic fever most often narrows it (mitral stenosis), giving a mid-diastolic murmur and atrial fibrillation.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-aortic"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Aortic valve" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Aortic valve"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Heart; Aortic valve is not separately marked" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">15</span><h3>Aortic valve</h3></div><small>Valva aortae</small><p>Three semilunar cusps (right, left and non-coronary) at the root of the aorta. The right and left sinuses above the cusps give off the coronary arteries.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Opens in systole, closes in diastole to prevent reflux into the left ventricle.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Calcific aortic stenosis in the elderly causes angina, syncope and breathlessness.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-pulmonary"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Pulmonary valve" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="500" data-point-y="316" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Pulmonary valve"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Heart teaching plate: Pulmonary valve" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 31.6%"></button><span class="number">16</span><h3>Pulmonary valve</h3></div><small>Valva trunci pulmonalis</small><p>Three semilunar cusps at the junction of the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Prevents backflow from the pulmonary trunk into the right ventricle during diastole.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Absent or stenotic in tetralogy of Fallot, contributing to right-to-left shunting.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-chordae"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Chordae tendineae" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="292" data-point-y="632" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Chordae tendineae"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Heart teaching plate: Chordae tendineae" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:29.2% 63.2%"></button><span class="number">17</span><h3>Chordae tendineae</h3></div><p>Fibrous &quot;heart strings&quot; running from the edges of the AV valve cusps to the papillary muscles.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Tauten during systole so the cusps cannot prolapse into the atria.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Rupture (e.g. after infarction or in endocarditis) causes sudden severe valve regurgitation.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-papillary"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Papillary muscles" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="400" data-point-y="737" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Papillary muscles"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Heart teaching plate: Papillary muscles" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:40.0% 73.7%"></button><span class="number">18</span><h3>Papillary muscles</h3></div><small>Musculi papillares</small><p>Conical muscles projecting from the ventricular walls; two in the left ventricle, three in the right.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Contract with the ventricle to anchor the chordae tendineae.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>The posteromedial papillary muscle has a single blood supply and is the one most likely to rupture after infarction.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-coronary"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Coronary arteries" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="542" data-point-y="305" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Coronary arteries"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Heart teaching plate: Coronary arteries" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:54.2% 30.5%"></button><span class="number">19</span><h3>Coronary arteries</h3></div><small>Arteriae coronariae</small><p>Right and left coronary arteries arising from the aortic sinuses. The left divides into the anterior interventricular (LAD) and circumflex branches.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Supply the myocardium itself — the heart fills during diastole but perfuses itself only then.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Atherosclerotic narrowing here causes angina; acute occlusion causes myocardial infarction. The LAD is the &quot;widow-maker&quot;.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-sa"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Sinoatrial node" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Sinoatrial node"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Heart; Sinoatrial node is not separately marked" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">20</span><h3>Sinoatrial node</h3></div><small>Nodus sinuatrialis</small><p>A crescent of specialised pacemaker cells in the right atrial wall at the entrance of the SVC. Fires at ~60–100/min without any nervous input.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Sets the heart rate — the natural pacemaker.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Sick sinus syndrome causes bradycardia and pauses, often needing a permanent pacemaker.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-av"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Atrioventricular node" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Atrioventricular node"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Heart; Atrioventricular node is not separately marked" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">21</span><h3>Atrioventricular node</h3></div><small>Nodus atrioventricularis</small><p>Pacemaker tissue in the inferior interatrial septum, near the coronary sinus and the septal cusp of the tricuspid valve.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Delays the impulse ~0.1 s so the atria finish emptying before the ventricles contract.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>The only normal electrical connection between atria and ventricles; block here causes heart block.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-his"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/heart.webp" data-part-name="Bundle of His &amp; bundle branches" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Bundle of His &amp; bundle branches"><img src="../../img/thumb/heart.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Heart; Bundle of His &amp; bundle branches is not separately marked" width="960" height="850" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">22</span><h3>Bundle of His &amp; bundle branches</h3></div><small>Fasciculus atrioventricularis</small><p>The bundle of His crosses the fibrous skeleton, then splits into right and left bundle branches that run down the interventricular septum to the Purkinje fibres.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Conducts the impulse rapidly to both ventricles so they contract almost simultaneously, from apex upwards.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Branch blocks widen the QRS complex on the ECG; the left bundle branch block masks infarction patterns.</p></div></section></div></section><section id="questions" class="content-section questions"><div class="eyebrow">Questions people ask</div><h2>Five questions about Heart</h2><p class="section-lead">Short answers grounded in the structures described above. Use the illustrated guide for anatomical context.</p><div class="qa-list"><div class="qa-item"><h3>How many chambers does the heart have?</h3><p>The heart has two upper receiving chambers, the right and left atria, and two lower pumping chambers, the right and left ventricles. The right ventricle sends blood to the lungs; the left sends it to the body.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>What route does blood take through the heart?</h3><p>Blood returns from the body to the right atrium, passes the tricuspid valve to the right ventricle, then crosses the pulmonary valve to the lungs. Oxygenated blood returns to the left atrium, passes the mitral valve to the left ventricle and leaves through the aortic valve.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>Why is the left ventricular wall thicker?</h3><p>The left ventricle must generate greater pressure to move blood through the systemic circulation. Its myocardium is therefore thicker than that of the right ventricle, which pumps into the lower-pressure pulmonary circulation.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>Do coronary arteries carry blood through the heart chambers?</h3><p>No. Coronary arteries branch near the aortic root and supply the heart muscle itself. The blood passing through the chambers does not directly nourish the full thickness of the myocardium.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>How do heart valves keep blood moving forward?</h3><p>Tricuspid and mitral valves guard the atrioventricular openings; pulmonary and aortic valves guard ventricular outflow. They open and close in response to pressure changes, limiting backward flow rather than actively pumping blood.</p></div></div></section><section id="related" class="content-section related-section"><div class="eyebrow">Keep exploring</div><h2>Related anatomy guides</h2><p class="section-lead">Follow these connected structures and body systems in the illustrated atlas.</p><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="../lungs/"><img src="../../img/thumb/lungs.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Lungs" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Lungs</strong><small>Two elastic, pleura-lined organs where air and blood come within 0.5 µm of each other.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../liver/"><img src="../../img/thumb/liver.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Liver" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Liver</strong><small>The body&#x27;s chemical plant: metabolism, bile, clotting factors, detoxification and storage.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../kidney/"><img src="../../img/thumb/kidney.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Kidney" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Kidney</strong><small>Coronal section plus a nephron: filtration, reabsorption, secretion and urine concentration.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../brain/"><img src="../../img/thumb/brain.webp" width="500" height="500" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Brain" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Brain</strong><small>Midsagittal section: cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem and cerebellum around the ventricular system.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a></div></section><section id="sources" class="content-section sources"><div class="eyebrow">Reference desk</div><h2>Further reading</h2><p class="section-lead">General anatomy references and related source chapters for continued study. These links do not make the digital illustrations clinical images or certify every anatomical variant.</p><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/19-1-heart-anatomy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li></ul><p class="disclaimer">This educational page is not medical advice and has not undergone formal clinical peer review. Spotted an error? <a href="../../about.html#contact">Contact the editor</a>.</p></section><div class="end-link"><div><strong>See the structures in context</strong><p>Open the realistic interactive plate, label lines, focus view and self-test.</p></div><a class="button" href="../../index.html#/heart">Open interactive view →</a></div></main><aside class="sidebar" aria-label="On this page"><div class="side-card"><h2>In this guide</h2><a href="#overview">Overview</a><a href="#parts">Illustrated structures (22)</a><a href="#questions">Five questions &amp; answers</a><a href="#related">Related guides</a><a href="#sources">Further reading</a></div><div class="side-card"><div class="count">20+</div><p>Documented anatomy and developmental entries in every Corpus chapter. Images are orientation aids, not clinical evidence.</p></div></aside></div><footer class="site-foot"><div class="shell foot-inner"><div><strong>Corpus Anatomy Atlas</strong><p>Illustrated anatomy for learning, not a diagnostic or surgical reference. Digital renders explain regions; they are not clinical photographs or substitutes for histology slides.</p></div><nav class="foot-links" aria-label="Footer"><a href="../../learn/">Study guides</a><a href="../../index.html">Interactive atlas</a><a href="../../about.html">About &amp; editorial policy</a><a href="../../privacy.html">Privacy</a></nav></div></footer><script defer src="../../reading.js?v=33"></script><script defer src="../../preview.js?v=48"></script></body></html>
