+<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="theme-color" content="#125e58"><title>Blastocyst implantation: endometrium, trophoblast and early hCG | Corpus</title><meta name="description" content="Understand hatching, apposition, attachment, cytotrophoblast, syncytiotrophoblast, decidua and early corpus luteum support."><meta name="robots" content="noindex,follow"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:title" content="Blastocyst implantation: endometrium, trophoblast and early hCG"><meta property="og:description" content="Understand hatching, apposition, attachment, cytotrophoblast, syncytiotrophoblast, decidua and early corpus luteum support."><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><link rel="icon" href="../../favicon.svg"><link rel="stylesheet" href="../../editorial.css?v=48"></head><body><a class="skip" href="#content">Skip to content</a><header class="site-head"><div class="shell head-inner"><a class="brand" href="../../index.html" aria-label="Corpus Anatomy Atlas home"><span class="brand-mark">♥</span><span class="brand-name">Corpus<small>Anatomy Atlas</small></span></a><nav class="head-nav" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="../../learn/" >Study guides</a><a href="../../index.html" >Interactive atlas</a><a href="../../index.html#/map" >Body map</a><a href="../../index.html#/quiz" >Quiz</a><a href="../../about.html" >About us</a></nav></div></header><div class="reading-track" aria-hidden="true"><span id="reading-progress"></span></div><nav class="crumb shell" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="../../index.html">Home</a><i>/</i><a href="../">Study guides</a><i>/</i><span>Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium</span></nav><section class="shell hero article-hero"><div class="eyebrow">Conception to birth · illustrated anatomy</div><h1>Blastocyst implantation: endometrium, trophoblast and early hCG</h1><p>Understand hatching, apposition, attachment, cytotrophoblast, syncytiotrophoblast, decidua and early corpus luteum support.</p><div class="action-row"><a class="button" href="../../index.html#/implantation">Open interactive atlas ↗</a><a class="button alt" href="#parts">Read the structures ↓</a></div><p class="byline">Corpus educational reference · 20 documented study entries · Updated 2026-10-19</p></section><div class="shell article-grid"><main id="content"><figure class="plate"><img src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" alt="Digital educational or regional anatomy illustration for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Preimplantation conceptual model; do not interpret it as already embedded." width="1264" height="689" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><figcaption>Regional teaching illustration. Preimplantation conceptual model; do not interpret it as already embedded. Preview a part for a more relevant close-up when available; only identifiable structures receive a pointer.</figcaption></figure><section id="overview"><div class="eyebrow">Overview</div><h2>How this part of the body works</h2><p class="intro-text">A hatched blastocyst attaches to receptive endometrium around the end of the first post-fertilization week, with timing variable. Its trophoblast differentiates into cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast, which interact with maternal tissue and begin early placental development; the inner cell mass gives rise to embryo proper. hCG supports the corpus luteum and progesterone early in pregnancy. Implantation within the uterine lining is distinct from normal fertilization in the uterine tube. The generated microanatomy is a conceptual section, not a patient biopsy or a claim about exact days. Ectopic implantation requires clinical assessment.</p><div class="facts"><div class="fact"><b>Body system</b><span>Conception to birth</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Location</b><span>Usually uterine endometrium near fundus or posterior wall</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Blood supply</b><span>Depends on testicular, ovarian, uterine and placental stage; see the named entries</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Innervation</b><span>Maternal pelvic autonomic and somatic pathways vary by structure; gametes have no nerve supply</span></div></div></section><section id="under-the-lens" class="content-section lens-reading"><div class="eyebrow">Microscopic-scale anatomy</div><h2>From organ to tissue</h2><p class="section-lead">These are educational cutaways, not literal magnifications of an organ pixel or laboratory slides. A ring marks the approximate tissue region; part-specific lines appear only where identifiable.</p><div class="lens-tiles"><article class="lens-tile"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger lens-tile-trigger" data-plate-preview data-part-name="Hatched blastocyst" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-lens-title="Blastocyst cutaway" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" aria-label="Enlarge organ-to-lens view of Hatched blastocyst"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Representative tissue cutaway for Hatched blastocyst" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></button><div class="lens-tile-copy"><small>ORGAN → UNDER THE LENS</small><h3>Blastocyst cutaway</h3><p>Tap to compare the organ region with a tissue-level teaching illustration.</p><a href="#part-hatched">Read about Hatched blastocyst ↓</a></div></article><article class="lens-tile"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger lens-tile-trigger" data-plate-preview data-part-name="Cytotrophoblast" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-placental-villus-micro.webp" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-lens-title="Chorionic villus · blood compartments" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" aria-label="Enlarge organ-to-lens view of Cytotrophoblast"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-placental-villus-micro.webp" width="450" height="251" alt="Representative tissue cutaway for Cytotrophoblast" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></button><div class="lens-tile-copy"><small>ORGAN → UNDER THE LENS</small><h3>Chorionic villus · blood compartments</h3><p>Tap to compare the organ region with a tissue-level teaching illustration.</p><a href="#part-cytotrophoblast">Read about Cytotrophoblast ↓</a></div></article></div></section><section id="repro-path" class="content-section repro-reading"><div class="eyebrow">Gametes to birth · separate chapters</div><h2>Follow every stage independently</h2><p class="section-lead">Sperm and egg are separate branches before fertilization. Obstetric gestational weeks are usually counted from the last menstrual period, roughly two weeks before conception. Digital labor-stage illustrations model separate moments, not clinical birth photographs or patient measurements.</p><nav class="repro-timeline" aria-label="Conception and childbirth study pathway"><a class="repro-stage " href="../spermanatomy/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-sperm.webp" width="450" height="251" alt="Regional educational context for Sperm cell" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>01 / 11</small><strong>Sperm cell</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../oocyteanatomy/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-oocyte.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Regional educational context for Egg / oocyte" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>02 / 11</small><strong>Egg / oocyte</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../fertilization/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-fertilization.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Regional educational context for Fertilization" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>03 / 11</small><strong>Fertilization</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../cleavageblastocyst/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Regional educational context for Early divisions" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>04 / 11</small><strong>Early divisions</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage selected" href="../implantation/" aria-current="page"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-implantation.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Regional educational context for Implantation" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>05 / 11</small><strong>Implantation</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../placentaanatomy/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-placenta.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Regional educational context for Placenta" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>06 / 11</small><strong>Placenta</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../embryodevelopment/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-embryo.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Regional educational context for Embryo" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>07 / 11</small><strong>Embryo</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../fetaldevelopment/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-fetus-second-trimester.webp" width="450" height="251" alt="Regional educational context for Fetus" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>08 / 11</small><strong>Fetus</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../laborfirststage/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-labor-stage-one.webp" width="450" height="251" alt="Regional educational context for Labor 1 · cervix" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>09 / 11</small><strong>Labor 1 · cervix</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../laborsecondstage/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-labor-stage-two.webp" width="450" height="251" alt="Regional educational context for Labor 2 · baby" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>10 / 11</small><strong>Labor 2 · baby</strong></span></a><a class="repro-stage " href="../laborthirdstage/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-labor-stage-three.webp" width="450" height="251" alt="Regional educational context for Labor 3 · placenta" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><small>11 / 11</small><strong>Labor 3 · placenta</strong></span></a></nav></section><section id="repro-gallery" class="content-section repro-gallery"><div class="eyebrow">Stage-specific picture library</div><h2>See the anatomy at the right scale</h2><p class="section-lead">Tap an image to enlarge and tap again to close. These are digital educational models, not microscope photos, measured ultrasounds, clinical birth images or exact developmental clocks.</p><div class="repro-gallery-grid"><figure class="repro-gallery-card"><button type="button" class="repro-gallery-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-part-name="Free blastocyst · before attachment" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" data-preview-note="Preimplantation conceptual model; do not interpret it as already embedded." aria-label="Enlarge Free blastocyst · before attachment"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Digital educational illustration: Free blastocyst · before attachment" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span aria-hidden="true">⤢</span></button><figcaption><small>01 / 3</small><strong>Free blastocyst · before attachment</strong><p>Preimplantation conceptual model; do not interpret it as already embedded.</p></figcaption></figure><figure class="repro-gallery-card"><button type="button" class="repro-gallery-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-part-name="Attachment within uterine lining" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" data-preview-note="Digital endometrial attachment cutaway; not a clinical implantation photograph." aria-label="Enlarge Attachment within uterine lining"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-implantation.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Digital educational illustration: Attachment within uterine lining" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span aria-hidden="true">⤢</span></button><figcaption><small>02 / 3</small><strong>Attachment within uterine lining</strong><p>Digital endometrial attachment cutaway; not a clinical implantation photograph.</p></figcaption></figure><figure class="repro-gallery-card"><button type="button" class="repro-gallery-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/endometrium-closeup.webp" data-part-name="Endometrial tissue · regional context" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" data-preview-note="Digital uterine lining model for glands and stroma; not a true sample from the pictured pregnancy." aria-label="Enlarge Endometrial tissue · regional context"><img src="../../img/thumb/endometrium-closeup.webp" width="420" height="229" alt="Digital educational illustration: Endometrial tissue · regional context" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span aria-hidden="true">⤢</span></button><figcaption><small>03 / 3</small><strong>Endometrial tissue · regional context</strong><p>Digital uterine lining model for glands and stroma; not a true sample from the pictured pregnancy.</p></figcaption></figure></div></section><section id="parts" class="content-section"><div class="eyebrow">Beyond the visible plate</div><h2>Stage anatomy and developmental milestones</h2><p class="section-lead">These 20 anatomy and development entries explain each stage without placing false pointers on an illustrative context plate.</p><div class="part-list"><section class="part" id="part-hatched"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-part-name="Hatched blastocyst" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-lens-title="Blastocyst cutaway" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Hatched blastocyst"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Hatched blastocyst; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">01</span><h3>Hatched blastocyst</h3></div><p>Zona-free blastocyst can directly interact with receptive endometrial surface.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Allows attachment and invasion.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Hatching alone does not establish an ongoing pregnancy.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Preimplantation conceptual model; do not interpret it as already embedded.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-endometrium"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/endometrium-closeup.webp" data-part-name="Secretory endometrium" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Secretory endometrium"><img src="../../img/thumb/endometrium-closeup.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Secretory endometrium is not separately marked" width="420" height="229" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">02</span><h3>Secretory endometrium</h3></div><p>Progesterone-conditioned uterine mucosa provides glands, vessels and receptive tissue.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Supports embryo–maternal interaction.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A nonpregnant uterus image is contextual, not confirmation of implantation.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Digital uterine lining model for glands and stroma; not a true sample from the pictured pregnancy.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-apposition"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-part-name="Apposition" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Apposition"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-implantation.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Apposition is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">03</span><h3>Apposition</h3></div><p>Initial loose positioning of blastocyst next to endometrial epithelium.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Precedes firmer attachment.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>The illustration cannot time this microscopic event exactly.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Digital endometrial attachment cutaway; not a clinical implantation photograph.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-adhesion"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-part-name="Adhesion" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Adhesion"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-implantation.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Adhesion is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">04</span><h3>Adhesion</h3></div><p>Trophoblast contacts and binds to receptive endometrial surface.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Begins stable implantation.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A visually adjacent sphere is not automatically attached.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Digital endometrial attachment cutaway; not a clinical implantation photograph.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-cytotrophoblast"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-placental-villus-micro.webp" data-part-name="Cytotrophoblast" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-lens-title="Chorionic villus · blood compartments" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Cytotrophoblast"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-placental-villus-micro.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Cytotrophoblast; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">05</span><h3>Cytotrophoblast</h3></div><p>Proliferative cellular trophoblast layer contributes cells to early placental structures.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Supports growth and differentiation of trophoblast.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Individual cell boundaries in this image are simplified.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> A related regional anatomy model; the individual event or cell is not separately identifiable in this picture.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-syncytiotrophoblast"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-placental-villus-micro.webp" data-part-name="Syncytiotrophoblast" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-lens-title="Chorionic villus · blood compartments" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Syncytiotrophoblast"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-placental-villus-micro.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Syncytiotrophoblast; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">06</span><h3>Syncytiotrophoblast</h3></div><p>Outer multinucleated trophoblast layer invades the decidualizing endometrium.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Anchors conceptus and participates in hormone production.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Do not treat the illustration as a diagnostic specimen.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> A related regional anatomy model; the individual event or cell is not separately identifiable in this picture.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-decidua"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/endometrium-closeup.webp" data-part-name="Decidualizing stromal cells" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Decidualizing stromal cells"><img src="../../img/thumb/endometrium-closeup.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Decidualizing stromal cells is not separately marked" width="420" height="229" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">07</span><h3>Decidualizing stromal cells</h3></div><p>Endometrial stromal cells transform under progesterone during early pregnancy.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Provide maternal support at implantation site.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Decidual change alone does not date pregnancy precisely.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Digital uterine lining model for glands and stroma; not a true sample from the pictured pregnancy.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-glands"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/endometrium-closeup.webp" data-part-name="Uterine glands" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Uterine glands"><img src="../../img/thumb/endometrium-closeup.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Uterine glands is not separately marked" width="420" height="229" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">08</span><h3>Uterine glands</h3></div><p>Endometrial glands remain part of receptive uterine mucosa.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Supply local secretions early in pregnancy.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A gland profile is not a developing embryo.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Digital uterine lining model for glands and stroma; not a true sample from the pictured pregnancy.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-maternalvessels"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/endometrium-closeup.webp" data-part-name="Maternal endometrial vessels" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Maternal endometrial vessels"><img src="../../img/thumb/endometrium-closeup.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Maternal endometrial vessels is not separately marked" width="420" height="229" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">09</span><h3>Maternal endometrial vessels</h3></div><p>Vessels within maternal uterine tissue remodel as placental interface evolves.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Provide later maternal blood to intervillous region.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Early vessels and established mature circulation are different stages.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Digital uterine lining model for glands and stroma; not a true sample from the pictured pregnancy.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-lacunae"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-part-name="Early trophoblastic lacunae" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Early trophoblastic lacunae"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-implantation.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Early trophoblastic lacunae is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">10</span><h3>Early trophoblastic lacunae</h3></div><p>Fluid spaces develop within syncytiotrophoblast during early interface formation.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Contribute to early placental exchange region.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>The plate is schematic, not a measured maternal blood-flow map.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Digital endometrial attachment cutaway; not a clinical implantation photograph.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-hcg"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-part-name="Human chorionic gonadotropin" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Human chorionic gonadotropin"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-implantation.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Human chorionic gonadotropin is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">11</span><h3>Human chorionic gonadotropin</h3></div><p>Trophoblast-derived hCG acts to preserve corpus luteum in early pregnancy.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Helps sustain progesterone support.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>hCG testing needs actual clinical sampling.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Digital endometrial attachment cutaway; not a clinical implantation photograph.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-corpusluteum"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-ovary-micro.webp" data-part-name="Corpus luteum support" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Corpus luteum support"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-ovary-micro.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Corpus luteum support is not separately marked" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">12</span><h3>Corpus luteum support</h3></div><p>Ovarian progesterone secretion persists in early pregnancy under hCG influence.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Maintains receptive endometrium before placental steroid output predominates.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>No corpus luteum is visible on this uterine microsection.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> A related regional anatomy model; the individual event or cell is not separately identifiable in this picture.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-innercell"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-blastocyst.webp" data-part-name="Inner cell mass" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Inner cell mass"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Inner cell mass is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">13</span><h3>Inner cell mass</h3></div><p>Blastocyst cell group away from trophoblast contributes to embryo proper.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Seeds embryonic tissues.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Not the same tissue as maternal uterine glands.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Preimplantation conceptual model; do not interpret it as already embedded.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-epiblast"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-part-name="Epiblast" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Epiblast"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-implantation.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Epiblast is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">14</span><h3>Epiblast</h3></div><p>Early embryonic cell population derived from inner cell mass.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Contributes to embryo proper and amniotic lineage.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Detailed bilaminar boundaries are not identifiable on this conceptual plate.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Digital endometrial attachment cutaway; not a clinical implantation photograph.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-hypoblast"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-part-name="Hypoblast" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Hypoblast"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-implantation.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Hypoblast is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">15</span><h3>Hypoblast</h3></div><p>Early bilaminar cell population related to extraembryonic endoderm formation.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Contributes to early yolk-sac lining.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Do not claim precise cell-level visualization here.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Digital endometrial attachment cutaway; not a clinical implantation photograph.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-amnion"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-part-name="Early amnion" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Early amnion"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-implantation.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Early amnion is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">16</span><h3>Early amnion</h3></div><p>Membrane and cavity start to develop adjacent to embryonic cells during early weeks.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Provides future protective fluid environment.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Do not mistake blastocoel for the mature amniotic sac.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Digital endometrial attachment cutaway; not a clinical implantation photograph.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-yolksac"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-part-name="Early yolk sac" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Early yolk sac"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-implantation.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Early yolk sac is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">17</span><h3>Early yolk sac</h3></div><p>Extraembryonic cavity forms early and supports developmental processes.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Participates in early nutrient transfer and blood formation.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Does not mean a human pregnancy depends on an avian-style food-filled yolk.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Digital endometrial attachment cutaway; not a clinical implantation photograph.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-chorion"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-part-name="Early chorion" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Early chorion"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-implantation.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Early chorion is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">18</span><h3>Early chorion</h3></div><p>Extraembryonic membrane derives from trophoblast-associated layers.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Contributes to fetal side of developing placenta.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Not fully developed placenta at first attachment.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Digital endometrial attachment cutaway; not a clinical implantation photograph.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-ectopic"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/gyn-adnexa.webp" data-part-name="Ectopic implantation" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Ectopic implantation"><img src="../../img/thumb/gyn-adnexa.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Ectopic implantation is not separately marked" width="960" height="524" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">19</span><h3>Ectopic implantation</h3></div><p>Blastocyst may implant outside the uterine cavity, commonly in a uterine tube.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Explains why normal tube fertilization must be followed by uterine transport.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Suspected ectopic pregnancy with pain or bleeding requires urgent assessment.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> A related regional anatomy model; the individual event or cell is not separately identifiable in this picture.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-dating"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/repro-implantation.webp" data-part-name="Implantation time variation" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Implantation time variation"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-implantation.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium; Implantation time variation is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">20</span><h3>Implantation time variation</h3></div><p>Attachment commonly starts roughly one week after fertilization, with individual variation.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Separates postfertilization age from LMP-based obstetric age.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A digital image cannot date an individual pregnancy.</p><p class="repro-part-caption"><b>Picture context</b> Digital endometrial attachment cutaway; not a clinical implantation photograph.</p></div></section></div></section><section id="questions" class="content-section questions"><div class="eyebrow">Questions people ask</div><h2>Five questions about Implantation · blastocyst in endometrium</h2><p class="section-lead">Short answers grounded in the structures described above. Use the illustrated guide for anatomical context.</p><div class="qa-list"><div class="qa-item"><h3>Where does a blastocyst normally implant?</h3><p>It typically attaches to receptive uterine endometrium, often in the upper or posterior uterus. Implantation within a uterine tube is ectopic and requires medical assessment.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>When does implantation begin after fertilization?</h3><p>Attachment commonly begins around the end of the first post-fertilization week, with variation. This is not the same as obstetric weeks counted from the last menstrual period.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>What is the difference between cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast?</h3><p>Cytotrophoblast is a cellular proliferative component; the outer syncytiotrophoblast is multinucleated and invades the endometrium. Both are early trophoblastic components, not a mature placenta by themselves.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>What does early pregnancy hCG support?</h3><p>Trophoblast-derived hCG maintains the ovarian corpus luteum so it continues progesterone production early in pregnancy. Later placental hormone production develops as the pregnancy progresses.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>Are uterine implantation and tubal fertilization the same event?</h3><p>No. Gamete fusion normally happens in a uterine tube. The conceptus then develops through divisions and reaches the uterus before a hatched blastocyst attaches to endometrium.</p></div></div></section><section id="related" class="content-section related-section"><div class="eyebrow">Keep exploring</div><h2>Related anatomy guides</h2><p class="section-lead">Follow these connected structures and body systems in the illustrated atlas.</p><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="../cleavageblastocyst/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-blastocyst.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Zygote to blastocyst · early divisions</strong><small>Trace the separate preimplantation chapter: zygote, cleavage, morula, blastocoel and hatching.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../placentaanatomy/"><img src="../../img/thumb/repro-placenta.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Placenta · maternal–fetal exchange" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Placenta · maternal–fetal exchange</strong><small>Separate placenta chapter: chorionic villi, decidua, separated circulations, cord and membranes.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../gynuterus/"><img src="../../img/thumb/gyn-uterus.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Uterus — layers &amp; cavity" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Uterus — layers &amp; cavity</strong><small>The womb in cross-section: endometrium, junctional zone, myometrium, cavity, isthmus and the key clinical boundaries.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../femalepelvis/"><img src="../../img/thumb/female-pelvis.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Female pelvis &amp; perineum" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Female pelvis &amp; perineum</strong><small>The female pelvic floor in sagittal section: bladder and urethra, vagina and anteverted uterus, rectum behind — plus the ligaments and pouches that position them.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a></div></section><section id="sources" class="content-section sources"><div class="eyebrow">Reference desk</div><h2>Further reading</h2><p class="section-lead">General anatomy references and related source chapters for continued study. These links do not make the digital illustrations clinical images or certify every anatomical variant.</p><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/28-2-embryonic-development" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/pediatrics/perinatal-problems/gestational-age" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">msdmanuals.com ↗</a></li></ul><p class="disclaimer">This educational page is not medical advice and has not undergone formal clinical peer review. Spotted an error? <a href="../../about.html#contact">Contact the editor</a>.</p></section><div class="end-link"><div><strong>See the structures in context</strong><p>Open the regional digital plate, study entries and a short self-test. Visible markers occur only for identifiable sperm and oocyte structures.</p></div><a class="button" href="../../index.html#/implantation">Open interactive view →</a></div></main><aside class="sidebar" aria-label="On this page"><div class="side-card"><h2>In this guide</h2><a href="#overview">Overview</a><a href="#parts">Study entries (20)</a><a href="#under-the-lens">Under the lens</a><a href="#repro-path">Conception to birth path</a><a href="#repro-gallery">Picture library</a><a href="#questions">Five questions &amp; answers</a><a href="#related">Related guides</a><a href="#sources">Further reading</a></div><div class="side-card"><div class="count">20+</div><p>Documented anatomy and developmental entries in every Corpus chapter. Images are orientation aids, not clinical evidence.</p></div></aside></div><footer class="site-foot"><div class="shell foot-inner"><div><strong>Corpus Anatomy Atlas</strong><p>Illustrated anatomy for learning, not a diagnostic or surgical reference. Digital renders explain regions; they are not clinical photographs or substitutes for histology slides.</p></div><nav class="foot-links" aria-label="Footer"><a href="../../learn/">Study guides</a><a href="../../index.html">Interactive atlas</a><a href="../../about.html">About &amp; editorial policy</a><a href="../../privacy.html">Privacy</a></nav></div></footer><script defer src="../../reading.js?v=33"></script><script defer src="../../preview.js?v=48"></script></body></html>
