+<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="theme-color" content="#125e58"><title>Four basic tissue types and cell anatomy: illustrated foundations | Corpus</title><meta name="description" content="Explore cell components and epithelial, connective, skeletal-muscle and nervous examples through four separate microscopic-scale teaching cutaways."><meta name="robots" content="noindex,follow"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:title" content="Four basic tissue types and cell anatomy: illustrated foundations"><meta property="og:description" content="Explore cell components and epithelial, connective, skeletal-muscle and nervous examples through four separate microscopic-scale teaching cutaways."><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><link rel="icon" href="../../favicon.svg"><link rel="stylesheet" href="../../editorial.css?v=48"></head><body><a class="skip" href="#content">Skip to content</a><header class="site-head"><div class="shell head-inner"><a class="brand" href="../../index.html" aria-label="Corpus Anatomy Atlas home"><span class="brand-mark">♥</span><span class="brand-name">Corpus<small>Anatomy Atlas</small></span></a><nav class="head-nav" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="../../learn/" >Study guides</a><a href="../../index.html" >Interactive atlas</a><a href="../../index.html#/map" >Body map</a><a href="../../index.html#/quiz" >Quiz</a><a href="../../about.html" >About us</a></nav></div></header><div class="reading-track" aria-hidden="true"><span id="reading-progress"></span></div><nav class="crumb shell" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="../../index.html">Home</a><i>/</i><a href="../">Study guides</a><i>/</i><span>Cells &amp; four basic tissues</span></nav><section class="shell hero article-hero"><div class="eyebrow">Anatomy foundations · illustrated anatomy</div><h1>Four basic tissue types and cell anatomy: illustrated foundations</h1><p>Explore cell components and epithelial, connective, skeletal-muscle and nervous examples through four separate microscopic-scale teaching cutaways.</p><div class="action-row"><a class="button" href="../../index.html#/tissuefoundations">Open interactive atlas ↗</a><a class="button alt" href="#parts">Read the structures ↓</a></div><p class="byline">Corpus educational reference · 28 documented study entries · Updated 2026-10-19</p></section><div class="shell article-grid"><main id="content"><figure class="plate"><img src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" alt="Digital educational or regional anatomy illustration for Cells &amp; four basic tissues; " width="1264" height="689" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><figcaption>Regional teaching illustration.  Preview a part for a more relevant close-up when available; only identifiable structures receive a pointer.</figcaption></figure><section id="overview"><div class="eyebrow">Overview</div><h2>How this part of the body works</h2><p class="intro-text">Cells organize into tissues, and organs contain combinations of epithelial, connective, muscular and nervous tissues. The main image illustrates a single layer of columnar epithelium above connective tissue; epithelium itself has no capillaries. Separate tissue-scale digital cutaways illustrate loose areolar connective tissue, skeletal muscle and a multipolar neuron. Cell organelles and some specialized tissue subtypes are described in text rather than falsely pinned on these images. These are computer-generated educational models, not histology slides, quantified microscopy or diagnostic samples.</p><div class="facts"><div class="fact"><b>Body system</b><span>Anatomy foundations</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Location</b><span>Cell-to-tissue scale across organs; epithelial example shown on main plate</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Blood supply</b><span>Varies by organ and tissue; no single foundation-specific blood supply</span></div><div class="fact"><b>Innervation</b><span>Varies by structure; directional terms have no nerve supply</span></div></div></section><section id="tissue-gallery" class="content-section foundation-reading"><div class="eyebrow">Cell and tissue scale</div><h2>Four different tissue families</h2><p class="section-lead">Digital microscopic-scale teaching cutaways, not real histology slides. Each plate shows a particular example, not every subtype.</p><div class="foundation-tissue-grid"><article class="foundation-panel"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger foundation-image" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-part-name="Epithelial tissue · teaching cutaway" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge Epithelial tissue teaching model"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-epithelium.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Digital epithelial tissue teaching cutaway" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></button><h3>Epithelial</h3><p>Simple columnar example</p></article><article class="foundation-panel"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger foundation-image" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-connective.webp" data-part-name="Connective tissue · teaching cutaway" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge Connective tissue teaching model"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-connective.webp" width="450" height="251" alt="Digital connective tissue teaching cutaway" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></button><h3>Connective</h3><p>Loose areolar example</p></article><article class="foundation-panel"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger foundation-image" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-skeletal-muscle.webp" data-part-name="Muscle tissue · teaching cutaway" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge Muscle tissue teaching model"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-skeletal-muscle.webp" width="450" height="251" alt="Digital muscle tissue teaching cutaway" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></button><h3>Muscle</h3><p>Skeletal muscle example</p></article><article class="foundation-panel"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger foundation-image" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-neural.webp" data-part-name="Nervous tissue · teaching cutaway" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge Nervous tissue teaching model"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-neural.webp" width="450" height="251" alt="Digital nervous tissue teaching cutaway" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></button><h3>Nervous</h3><p>Multipolar neuron model</p></article></div></section><section id="parts" class="content-section"><div class="eyebrow">Illustrated structures</div><h2>Read the anatomy, part by part</h2><p class="section-lead">These 2 structures describe anatomy related to the chapter’s regional illustration. Each entry separates location, function and a clinical point.</p><div class="part-list"><section class="part" id="part-epithelium"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-part-name="Epithelial tissue" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="510" data-point-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Epithelial tissue"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-epithelium.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Cells &amp; four basic tissues teaching plate: Epithelial tissue" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:51.0% 38.0%"></button><span class="number">01</span><h3>Epithelial tissue</h3></div><p>Close-packed cell sheets cover body surfaces, line passages and form much glandular tissue; the image depicts one simple columnar example.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Provides selective barrier, absorption, transport and secretion.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>This is a digital teaching cutaway, not a patient histology slide or a picture of every epithelium.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-basallamina"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-part-name="Basal lamina (basement-membrane zone)" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="false" data-point-x="510" data-point-y="710" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Basal lamina (basement-membrane zone)"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-epithelium.webp" alt="Visible structure on the Cells &amp; four basic tissues teaching plate: Basal lamina (basement-membrane zone)" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:51.0% 71.0%"></button><span class="number">02</span><h3>Basal lamina (basement-membrane zone)</h3></div><p>Specialized extracellular layer beneath epithelial cells, shown as a fine strip above vascular connective tissue.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Anchors and organizes the epithelial sheet.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Basement membrane in tissue sections often includes more than the basal lamina alone.</p></div></section></div></section><section id="deep" class="content-section"><div class="eyebrow">Beyond the visible plate</div><h2>Terminology and study entries</h2><p class="section-lead">These 26 additional entries give anatomical context. They are deliberately not assigned a photographic pin on the regional image.</p><div class="part-list"><section class="part" id="part-cell"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-cell-organelles.webp" data-part-name="Human cell" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Cell and organelles" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cellular-level-of-organization" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Human cell"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-cell-organelles.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Human cell; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">03</span><h3>Human cell</h3></div><p>A cell is a membrane-bound living unit with internal components that perform metabolism and communication.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Combines organelles to maintain tissue function.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A cutaway is a stylized model, not a universal cell portrait.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-membrane"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-cell-organelles.webp" data-part-name="Plasma membrane" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Cell and organelles" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cellular-level-of-organization" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Plasma membrane"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-cell-organelles.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Plasma membrane; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">04</span><h3>Plasma membrane</h3></div><p>Lipid bilayer with embedded proteins separates cell interior from extracellular fluid.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Regulates exchange, signaling and cellular identity.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Its molecular bilayer cannot be directly resolved on a gross organ plate.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-cytoplasm"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-cell-organelles.webp" data-part-name="Cytoplasm and cytosol" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Cell and organelles" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cellular-level-of-organization" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Cytoplasm and cytosol"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-cell-organelles.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Cytoplasm and cytosol; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">05</span><h3>Cytoplasm and cytosol</h3></div><p>Cytoplasm includes cytosol and organelles outside the nucleus; cytosol is the fluid component.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Hosts much of cellular metabolism.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Do not treat the two terms as exact synonyms.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-nucleus"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-cell-organelles.webp" data-part-name="Nucleus" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Cell and organelles" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cellular-level-of-organization" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Nucleus"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-cell-organelles.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Nucleus; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">06</span><h3>Nucleus</h3></div><p>Membrane-enclosed compartment holding most nuclear DNA in many human cell types.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Coordinates gene expression and cell division.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Mature red blood cells lack a nucleus; visible ovals on this plate are idealized epithelial nuclei.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-mitochondria"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-cell-organelles.webp" data-part-name="Mitochondria" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Cell and organelles" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cellular-level-of-organization" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Mitochondria"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-cell-organelles.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Mitochondria; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">07</span><h3>Mitochondria</h3></div><p>Double-membrane organelles that generate much cellular ATP through oxidative phosphorylation.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Provide energy for many cellular activities.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Mitochondrial shapes on this digital cell model are illustrative, not microscopy.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-ribosome"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-cell-organelles.webp" data-part-name="Ribosomes and rough ER" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Cell and organelles" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cellular-level-of-organization" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Ribosomes and rough ER"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-cell-organelles.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Ribosomes and rough ER; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">08</span><h3>Ribosomes and rough ER</h3></div><p>Ribosomes translate messenger RNA; those bound to rough endoplasmic reticulum make many secreted and membrane proteins.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Supports protein synthesis and processing.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Ribosome dots on this teaching model are not individually identifiable microscopy.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-golgi"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-cell-organelles.webp" data-part-name="Golgi apparatus" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Cell and organelles" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cellular-level-of-organization" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Golgi apparatus"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-cell-organelles.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Golgi apparatus; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">09</span><h3>Golgi apparatus</h3></div><p>Stacked membrane compartments that modify and sort proteins and lipids.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Directs cargo toward secretion, membrane or lysosomal pathways.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>The colored Golgi-like stack is a digital teaching aid, not a clinical specimen.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-matrix"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-connective.webp" data-part-name="Extracellular matrix" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Loose areolar connective tissue" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/4-3-connective-tissue-supports-and-protects" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Extracellular matrix"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-connective.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Extracellular matrix; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">10</span><h3>Extracellular matrix</h3></div><p>Noncellular network of fibrous proteins and hydrated ground substance outside connective-tissue cells.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Supports tissues and influences cell signaling.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Its composition varies between areolar tissue, cartilage, tendon and bone.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-apical"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-part-name="Apical surface and cell polarity" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Apical surface and cell polarity"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-epithelium.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Cells &amp; four basic tissues; Apical surface and cell polarity is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">11</span><h3>Apical surface and cell polarity</h3></div><p>The free epithelial cell surface faces a lumen or environment, while the basal side faces the underlying support.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Allows directional absorption, secretion and barrier function.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Apical microvilli on the plate are schematic and cannot classify a specimen alone.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-simple"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-part-name="Simple versus stratified epithelium" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Simple versus stratified epithelium"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-epithelium.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Cells &amp; four basic tissues; Simple versus stratified epithelium is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">12</span><h3>Simple versus stratified epithelium</h3></div><p>Simple epithelium has one cell layer; stratified epithelium has multiple layers. This plate depicts a simple arrangement.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Relates layer number to transport or protective roles.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Pseudostratified epithelium appears layered but every cell contacts the basal lamina.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-connective"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-connective.webp" data-part-name="Connective tissue" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Loose areolar connective tissue" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/4-3-connective-tissue-supports-and-protects" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Connective tissue"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-connective.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Connective tissue; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">13</span><h3>Connective tissue</h3></div><p>Cells are dispersed in an extracellular matrix; the companion plate illustrates loose areolar connective tissue.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Supports and integrates tissues, with many specialized forms.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Bone, cartilage and blood are distinct connective-tissue forms, not the same as areolar tissue.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-fibroblast"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-connective.webp" data-part-name="Fibroblast" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Loose areolar connective tissue" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/4-3-connective-tissue-supports-and-protects" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Fibroblast"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-connective.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Fibroblast; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">14</span><h3>Fibroblast</h3></div><p>Spindle-shaped cells synthesize components of connective extracellular matrix, including collagen.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Maintains and remodels connective tissue.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Digital cell shapes alone cannot substitute for a histologic diagnosis.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-collagen"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-connective.webp" data-part-name="Collagen fibers" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Loose areolar connective tissue" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/4-3-connective-tissue-supports-and-protects" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Collagen fibers"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-connective.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Collagen fibers; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">15</span><h3>Collagen fibers</h3></div><p>Protein fibers provide tensile support; loose areolar tissue contains scattered wavy bundles.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Resists stretch and mechanical load.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Fiber amount and type change with tissue and disease.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-capillary"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-connective.webp" data-part-name="Connective-tissue capillary" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Loose areolar connective tissue" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/4-3-connective-tissue-supports-and-protects" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Connective-tissue capillary"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-connective.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Connective-tissue capillary; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">16</span><h3>Connective-tissue capillary</h3></div><p>Small vessel in supporting connective tissue carries blood near, not into, the epithelial sheet.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Supplies nutrients that diffuse toward avascular epithelium.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>This schematic is not a scale-accurate microvascular map.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-muscle"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-skeletal-muscle.webp" data-part-name="Muscle tissue" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Skeletal muscle fibres" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/10-2-skeletal-muscle" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Muscle tissue"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-skeletal-muscle.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Muscle tissue; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">17</span><h3>Muscle tissue</h3></div><p>Excitable cells shorten and produce force; skeletal, cardiac and smooth muscle are three distinct types.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Supports movement, circulation and organ wall function.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>The accompanying striated plate depicts skeletal muscle only.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-skeletal"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-skeletal-muscle.webp" data-part-name="Skeletal muscle fiber" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Skeletal muscle fibres" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/10-2-skeletal-muscle" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Skeletal muscle fiber"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-skeletal-muscle.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Skeletal muscle fiber; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">18</span><h3>Skeletal muscle fiber</h3></div><p>Long multinucleated, striated contractile cell with peripheral nuclei in normal skeletal muscle.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Produces voluntary skeletal movement.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>The illustrated longitudinal section should not be confused with branching cardiac muscle.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-endomysium"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-skeletal-muscle.webp" data-part-name="Endomysium" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Skeletal muscle fibres" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/10-2-skeletal-muscle" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Endomysium"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-skeletal-muscle.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Endomysium; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">19</span><h3>Endomysium</h3></div><p>Thin connective-tissue layer surrounding each individual skeletal muscle fiber.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Supports small vessels and transmits contractile forces.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Perimysium instead surrounds bundles of fibers.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-cardiac"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-cardiac-muscle.webp" data-part-name="Cardiac muscle" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Cardiac muscle tissue" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/19-2-cardiac-muscle-and-electrical-activity" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Cardiac muscle"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-cardiac-muscle.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Cardiac muscle; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">20</span><h3>Cardiac muscle</h3></div><p>Striated, typically branched cardiomyocytes join through intercalated discs in the heart.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Generates coordinated cardiac pumping.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>This illustration is a stylized cardiac example, not a diagnostic histology slide.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-smooth"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-smooth-muscle.webp" data-part-name="Smooth muscle" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Smooth muscle tissue" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/10-8-smooth-muscle" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Smooth muscle"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-smooth-muscle.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Smooth muscle; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">21</span><h3>Smooth muscle</h3></div><p>Nonstriated spindle-shaped contractile cells occur in walls of many hollow organs and vessels.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Controls lumen diameter and organ-wall motion.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>This plate is a stylized smooth-muscle example, not diagnostic histology.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-nervoustissue"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-neural.webp" data-part-name="Nervous tissue" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Neuron and glial support" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/12-2-nervous-tissue" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Nervous tissue"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-neural.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Nervous tissue; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">22</span><h3>Nervous tissue</h3></div><p>Neurons and supporting glia form excitable networks in central and peripheral nervous systems.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Detects, processes and transmits information.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Illustrated neuron and myelin are teaching-scale models, not micrographs.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-neuron"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-neural.webp" data-part-name="Neuron cell body and dendrites" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Neuron and glial support" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/12-2-nervous-tissue" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Neuron cell body and dendrites"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-neural.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Neuron cell body and dendrites; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">23</span><h3>Neuron cell body and dendrites</h3></div><p>Soma houses cell machinery and receives signals through branching dendrites; form varies with neuron class.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Integrates synaptic inputs.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>A multipolar example cannot represent all neuronal morphologies.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-axon"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-neural.webp" data-part-name="Axon" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Neuron and glial support" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/12-2-nervous-tissue" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Axon"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-neural.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Axon; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">24</span><h3>Axon</h3></div><p>Single main output process carries electrical impulses away from a neuron’s initial segment; it may have collaterals.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Communicates toward synaptic targets.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>The illustrated path is conceptual, not a clinical nerve map.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-myelin"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-neural.webp" data-part-name="Myelin sheath and nodes" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Neuron and glial support" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/12-2-nervous-tissue" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Myelin sheath and nodes"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-neural.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Myelin sheath and nodes; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">25</span><h3>Myelin sheath and nodes</h3></div><p>Repeated myelin segments electrically insulate portions of some axons, leaving short gaps called nodes of Ranvier.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Supports rapid saltatory conduction.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Not every axon is myelinated and different glia myelinate CNS and PNS axons.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-glia"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-neural.webp" data-part-name="Glial support cells" data-preview-kind="scope" data-preview-context="true" data-overview-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-lens-title="Neuron and glial support" data-source-url="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/12-2-nervous-tissue" data-origin-x="500" data-origin-y="380" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Glial support cells"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-neural.webp" alt="Relevant tissue-level illustration for Glial support cells; individual structure not singled out" width="450" height="251" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">26</span><h3>Glial support cells</h3></div><p>Astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, microglia and Schwann cells have different CNS or PNS roles.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Support neurons, homeostasis and myelin formation.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>The small background cells here are generic, not separately identifiable glial subtypes.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-fourtypes"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-part-name="Four fundamental tissue categories" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Four fundamental tissue categories"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-epithelium.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Cells &amp; four basic tissues; Four fundamental tissue categories is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">27</span><h3>Four fundamental tissue categories</h3></div><p>Epithelial, connective, muscle and nervous tissue are the four broad human tissue families.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Provides a framework for reading tissue architecture.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>An organ usually contains more than one tissue type.</p></div></section><section class="part" id="part-histology"><div class="part-head"><button type="button" class="reading-mini-trigger" data-plate-preview data-plate-src="../../img/webp/foundation-epithelium.webp" data-part-name="Histology versus digital cutaway" data-preview-kind="region" data-preview-context="true" aria-label="Enlarge anatomy image for Histology versus digital cutaway"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-epithelium.webp" alt="Regional reference plate for Cells &amp; four basic tissues; Histology versus digital cutaway is not separately marked" width="450" height="245" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="object-position:50.0% 50.0%"></button><span class="number">28</span><h3>Histology versus digital cutaway</h3></div><p>Histology examines prepared tissue sections with microscopy; these four generated images are stylized educational cutaways.</p><div class="detail"><p><b>What it does</b>Distinguishes tissue concepts from diagnostic specimens.</p><p><b>Clinical connection</b>Never diagnose disease or claim real staining or magnification from these images.</p></div></section></div></section><section id="questions" class="content-section questions"><div class="eyebrow">Questions people ask</div><h2>Five questions about Cells &amp; four basic tissues</h2><p class="section-lead">Short answers grounded in the structures described above. Use the illustrated guide for anatomical context.</p><div class="qa-list"><div class="qa-item"><h3>What are the four basic types of tissue?</h3><p>The broad categories are epithelial, connective, muscle and nervous tissue. Most organs contain multiple types; the images show specific teaching examples, not all subtypes.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>What makes epithelial tissue different from connective tissue?</h3><p>Epithelia form close-packed sheets with apical and basal surfaces and little extracellular space. Connective-tissue cells are often dispersed in extracellular matrix. Epithelium is generally avascular and nourished from underlying tissues.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>Are these four pictures actual microscope slides?</h3><p>No. They are digitally generated microscopic-scale educational cutaways rather than histology specimens, patient images, measured magnifications or diagnostic evidence.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>Which muscle type is shown in the foundation plate?</h3><p>The striated, multinucleated parallel fibers depict a skeletal-muscle example. Smooth muscle is not striated, and cardiac muscle differs in branching and intercalated discs; neither has a dedicated picture in this batch.</p></div><div class="qa-item"><h3>What structures make up nervous tissue?</h3><p>Nervous tissue contains signaling neurons and supporting glial cells. The example shows a multipolar neuron with dendrites, an axon and myelin on some axon segments, not a map of a named clinical nerve.</p></div></div></section><section id="related" class="content-section related-section"><div class="eyebrow">Keep exploring</div><h2>Related anatomy guides</h2><p class="section-lead">Follow these connected structures and body systems in the illustrated atlas.</p><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="../anatomicallanguage/"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-planes.webp" width="274" height="340" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Anatomical language · planes &amp; movement" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Anatomical language · planes &amp; movement</strong><small>Learn patient right and left, body planes, directional terms and movements without false image pins.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../bodyregions/"><img src="../../img/thumb/foundation-body-cavities.webp" width="450" height="245" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Body cavities · regions &amp; quadrants" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Body cavities · regions &amp; quadrants</strong><small>Trace dorsal and ventral cavities, serous spaces and patient-oriented abdominal quadrants and regions.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../skinlayers/"><img src="../../img/thumb/skin-layers-deep.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Skin layers &amp; appendages" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Skin layers &amp; appendages</strong><small>The five thick-skin epidermal strata, two dermal zones, hypodermis, receptors and follicle–gland units.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a><a class="related-card" href="../intestinalwall/"><img src="../../img/thumb/intestinal-wall-deep.webp" width="960" height="524" alt="Digital educational or regional context image for Small intestine · wall &amp; enteric plexuses" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><strong>Small intestine · wall &amp; enteric plexuses</strong><small>Map villus–crypt renewal, epithelial cell types, capillaries and lacteals, plus two enteric nerve plexuses.</small></span><b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a></div></section><section id="sources" class="content-section sources"><div class="eyebrow">Reference desk</div><h2>Further reading</h2><p class="section-lead">General anatomy references and related source chapters for continued study. These links do not make the digital illustrations clinical images or certify every anatomical variant.</p><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/details/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/4-1-types-of-tissues" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/4-2-epithelial-tissue" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/4-3-connective-tissue-supports-and-protects" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/12-2-nervous-tissue" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cellular-level-of-organization" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/10-2-skeletal-muscle" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/19-2-cardiac-muscle-and-electrical-activity" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/10-8-smooth-muscle" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openstax.org ↗</a></li></ul><p class="disclaimer">This educational page is not medical advice and has not undergone formal clinical peer review. Spotted an error? <a href="../../about.html#contact">Contact the editor</a>.</p></section><div class="end-link"><div><strong>See the structures in context</strong><p>Open the realistic interactive plate, label lines, focus view and self-test.</p></div><a class="button" href="../../index.html#/tissuefoundations">Open interactive view →</a></div></main><aside class="sidebar" aria-label="On this page"><div class="side-card"><h2>In this guide</h2><a href="#overview">Overview</a><a href="#parts">Illustrated structures (2)</a><a href="#deep">Deep study notes (26)</a><a href="#tissue-gallery">Four tissue images</a><a href="#questions">Five questions &amp; answers</a><a href="#related">Related guides</a><a href="#sources">Further reading</a></div><div class="side-card"><div class="count">20+</div><p>Documented anatomy and developmental entries in every Corpus chapter. Images are orientation aids, not clinical evidence.</p></div></aside></div><footer class="site-foot"><div class="shell foot-inner"><div><strong>Corpus Anatomy Atlas</strong><p>Illustrated anatomy for learning, not a diagnostic or surgical reference. Digital renders explain regions; they are not clinical photographs or substitutes for histology slides.</p></div><nav class="foot-links" aria-label="Footer"><a href="../../learn/">Study guides</a><a href="../../index.html">Interactive atlas</a><a href="../../about.html">About &amp; editorial policy</a><a href="../../privacy.html">Privacy</a></nav></div></footer><script defer src="../../reading.js?v=33"></script><script defer src="../../preview.js?v=48"></script></body></html>
